    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=191179&JobId=192910": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Aerodynamics and Unmanned Aerial Vehicle)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Aerodynamics and Unmanned Aerial Vehicle) Aerospace Engineering Location College Station, TX Open Date 9/16/2026 Position ID 191179 Description The Department of Aerospace Engineering, College of Engineering at Texas A&M University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track faculty position with a 9-month academic appointment, with the possibility of an additional summer appointment contingent upon the needs and availability of funds, beginning the Fall of 2027. Applicants will be considered at the level of Assistant Professor. Candidates will be considered in two broad areas: 1) Urban air mobility and UAVs. While applications from all related areas will be considered, the department is particularly interested in candidates with a strong record in experimental work, aerodynamics, and propulsion. 2) Reacting flows. While applications from all related areas will be considered, the department is particularly interested in candidates with a strong record on experimental work. Successful applicants will be required to teach; advise and mentor graduate students; develop an independent externally funded research program at the forefront of aerospace engineering and sciences; participate in all aspects of the department’s activities; and serve the profession. Applicants should consult the department’s website to review our academic and research programs ( https://engineering.tamu.edu/aerospace ) in particular our experimental low-speed wind tunnels. Qualifications Applicants must have an earned doctorate in aerospace engineering or a closely related science discipline. Application Instructions Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a personal statement (to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service), and a list of four references (including postal addresses, phone numbers and email addresses) by applying for this specific position at https://apply.interfolio.com/192910 . Full consideration will be given to applications received by October 1, 2026. It is anticipated that the appointment will begin August 2027. Questions regarding this position should be directed to Lance Isenhour at lisenhour@tamu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=188951&JobId=190843": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Architectural Engineering – Building Mechanical Systems)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Architectural Engineering – Building Mechanical Systems) Civil and Environmental Engineering Location College Station, TX Open Date 8/20/2026 Position ID 188951 Description The ABET-accredited Architectural Engineering (AREN) program will join the Zachry Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering in the College of Engineering at Texas A&M University effective September 1, 2026. The Zachry Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position with a nine-month academic appointment. An additional summer appointment may be available, contingent upon departmental needs and the availability of funds. The anticipated start date is Spring 2027 or Fall 2027. Applicants will be considered at the Assistant Professor level with expertise in Architectural Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, or a closely related discipline, with a focus on Building Mechanical Systems. The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate strong fundamental knowledge in a core area of architectural engineering and the ability to apply this expertise to address societally relevant challenges. We seek candidates with expertise in one or more of the following areas: building HVAC systems; building energy modeling and simulation; high-performance and net-zero energy buildings; indoor environmental quality; sustainable and resilient building systems; thermal sciences as applied to buildings; smart buildings, building automation and controls; artificial intelligence/machine learning for intelligent building operation; renewable energy integration; district energy systems; and advanced sensing, diagnostics, and fault detection. Candidates should demonstrate rigorous training in their primary area of expertise, along with strong potential to contribute to the field through original scholarship, effective teaching and student mentoring, and service to the university and the profession. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses; advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students; develop an independent, externally funded research program; participate in departmental activities and service; collaborate with faculty across the College of Engineering; engage with industry and government partners and in professional service; and contribute to the continued growth of the Architectural Engineering program. The successful candidate will also contribute to undergraduate and graduate curriculum development, program assessment, and continuous improvement activities associated with maintaining excellence in the ABET-accredited Architectural Engineering program. Strong written and verbal communication skills are required. Applicants are encouraged to visit the Architectural Engineering program and the Zachry Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering websites to learn more about the department's academic and research programs, as well as the vision and mission of the College of Engineering. The Zachry Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering is among the leading civil and environmental engineering departments in the nation, with approximately 55 tenured/tenure-track faculty, 22 academic professional track faculty members. Our student population is comprised of over 900 undergraduate students (sophomore to senior level) and 370 graduate students. In addition, the Architectural Engineering program includes over 280 undergraduate students. For more information on the department and Architectural Engineering program, please visit http://engineering.tamu.edu/civil and https://engineering.tamu.edu/mtde/academics/degrees/undergraduate/architectural-engineering . Qualifications Applicants must have an earned doctoral degree in Architectural Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Civil Engineering, or a closely related engineering or science discipline by the appointment date. Candidates completing all Ph.D. requirements except the dissertation (ABD) will be considered, provided they demonstrate clear progress toward completion. Additional Required Qualifications: Strong background in thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, heat transfer, and building environmental systems. Potential to develop a nationally and internationally recognized, externally funded research program. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and student mentoring. Ability to collaborate across disciplines and with industry partners. Preferred Qualifications: Professional Engineering licensure or the ability to obtain licensure is desirable/preferred but not required. Application Instructions Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a personal statement (to include philosophy of teaching, research, and service), and a list of four references (including postal addresses, telephone numbers, and email addresses) through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/190843 . Full consideration will be given to applications received by September 15, 2026 . Applications received after that date may be considered until the position is filled. It is anticipated the appointment will begin Spring 2027 or Fall 2027 . Questions regarding the application process should be directed to Anna T. Kjolen (akjolen@tamu.edu). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=190591&JobId=192360": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Chemical Engineering, Metabolic Engineering and Bioprocessing)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Chemical Engineering, Metabolic Engineering and Bioprocessing) Chemical Engineering Location College Station, TX Open Date 8/26/2026 Position ID 190591 Description The Artie McFerrin Department of Chemical Engineering, College of Engineering at Texas A&M University invites applications for two full-time, tenure-track, assistant professor positions with a 9-month, academic appointment, and the possibility of an additional summer appointment contingent upon need and availability of funds, beginning Fall 2027. The principal focus for this position is in the area of metabolic engineering and bioprocess engineering. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research integrates the design, optimization, and scale-up of microbial and cell-based systems to enable sustainable production of biomaterials, platform chemicals as feedstocks for fuels and chemicals manufacturing, biopharmaceuticals including emerging modalities such as engineered proteins, nucleic acid-based therapeutics, and cell-free expression systems, and other high-value products. The successful candidate is expected to establish and sustain a nationally recognized, externally funded research program at the interface of metabolic engineering and bioprocess development. Areas of emphasis may include but are not limited to pathway and genome engineering, systems and computational biology, AI and machine learning tools and applications, digital twins and process analytical technologies enabling real-time monitoring and control, process intensification and continuous or modular bioprocessing, advanced bioreactor design, bioseparations, and technoeconomic or life cycle analysis of emerging biomanufacturing processes. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct original, scholary research; build self-sustaining research programs; teach both graduate and undergraduate courses and mentor students; and contribute an appropriate degree of service to the Department, College, University, and profession. Candidates must demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both undergraduate and graduate levels, as well as active engagement in departmental, college, and professional service. The position offers extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across Texas A&M’s strengths in engineering, life sciences, and agriculture, and aligns with university-wide investments in biomanufacturing and sustainability. The successful candidate will benefit from access to the department's growing capabilities in biomanufacturing, including the newly established biomanufacturing laboratories, ongoing activities within the National Center for Therapeutics Manufacturing (NCTM), and other university-wide investments supporting innovation in biomanufacturing and sustainability. Qualifications Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering, Bioengineering, or a closely related field, with an outstanding record of scholarly achievement, ability to secure competitive research funding, and evidence of effective teaching and mentorship. Application Instructions Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a personal Statement (your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service as applicable), and a list of four contact references (including postal addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses) by applying for this specific position at https://apply.interfolio.com/192360 . Full consideration will be given to applications received by December 15, 2026. Applications received after that date may be considered until the position is filled. It is anticipated the appointment will begin in Fall 2027. For questions regarding the application process or other inquiries, please contact Mr. Mateo Andres ( chenfacultyservices@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=190604&JobId=192370": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Chemical Engineering, Process Systems Engineering)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (Chemical Engineering, Process Systems Engineering) Chemical Engineering Location College Station, TX Open Date 8/26/2026 Position ID 190604 Description The Artie McFerrin Department of Chemical Engineering, College of Engineering at Texas A&M University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track, assistant professor position with a 9-month academic appointment and the possibility of an additional summer appointment contingent upon need and availability of funds, beginning Fall 2027. The principal focus of this position is in Process Systems Engineering. We seek candidates with strong expertise in one or more areas of computational and algorithmic methods; scientific artificial intelligence and machine learning; multiscale modeling and simulation; large-scale optimization; and hybrid, physics, and/or data-driven approaches for the design, operation, control, and decision-making under uncertainty in processing systems. Research applications may include sustainable chemicals, pharmaceuticals, and fuels, resilient energy systems, advanced manufacturing, the circular economy, and other emerging challenges in chemical and process systems engineering. The successful candidate is expected to establish and maintain a nationally recognized, externally funded research program at the intersection of process systems engineering, chemical engineering, and decision science. Research areas of interest include, but are not limited to, multiscale modeling; optimization; and computational methods for the design, operation, control, and decision-making under uncertainty in processing systems. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct original, scholary research; build self-sustaining research programs; teach both graduate and undergraduate courses and mentor students; and contribute an appropriate degree of service to the Department, College, University, and profession. Candidates must demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both undergraduate and graduate levels, as well as active engagement in departmental, college, and professional service. The position offers extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across Texas A&M’s strengths in engineering, life sciences, and agriculture, and aligns with university-wide investments in biomanufacturing and sustainability. Qualifications Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering, Process Systems Engineering, or a closely related field, with an outstanding record of scholarly achievement, ability to secure competitive research funding, and evidence of effective teaching and mentorship. Application Instructions Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a personal Statement (your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service as applicable), and a list of four contact references (including postal addresses, phone numbers and email addresses) by applying for this specific position at https://apply.interfolio.com/192370 . Full consideration will be given to applications received by December 15, 2026. Applications received after that date may be considered until the position is filled. It is anticipated the appointment will begin in Fall 2027. For questions regarding the application process or other inquiries, please contact Mr. Mateo Andres ( chenfacultyservices@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=190610&JobId=192372": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (CHEN, AI and Machine Learning in Chemical Engineering)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (CHEN, AI and Machine Learning in Chemical Engineering) Chemical Engineering Location College Station, TX Open Date 8/26/2026 Position ID 190610 Description The Artie McFerrin Department of Chemical Engineering , College of Engineering at Texas A&M University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track, assistant professor position with a 9-month academic appointment and the possibility of an additional summer appointment contingent upon need and availability of funds, beginning Fall 2027. The principal focus for this position is at the intersection of computational data science and Chemical Engineering. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research leverages Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) to advance process systems design, process safety and risk analysis, optimization, characterization, and predictive modeling of materials. The successful candidate is expected to establish and sustain a nationally recognized, externally funded research program that integrates computational data science with chemical engineering. Areas of emphasis may include, but are not limited to, data-driven process modeling and simulation of complex, large-scale systems; AI-accelerated materials design, including structure-property relationships, self-assembly, and processing optimization; machine learning-driven characterization and stabilization of colloidal systems; physics-informed machine learning; generative models for materials discovery; advanced ML and AI methods for process design, safety and risk analysis, and high-throughput computational screening methodologies addressing fundamental and applied challenges in chemical engineering. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct original, scholary research; build self-sustaining research programs; teach both graduate and undergraduate courses and mentor students; and contribute an appropriate degree of service to the Department, College, University, and profession. Candidates must demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both undergraduate and graduate levels, as well as active engagement in departmental, college, and professional service. The ideal candidate will articulate a clear vision for integrating experimental, theoretical, and data-driven approaches to solve complex problems at the forefront of chemical engineering research. Qualifications Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering or a closely related field, with an outstanding record of scholarly achievement, the ability to secure competitive research funding, and evidence of effective teaching and mentorship. Application Instructions Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a personal Statement (your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service as applicable), and a list of four contact references (including postal addresses, phone numbers and email addresses) by applying for this specific position at https://apply.interfolio.com/192372 . Full consideration will be given to applications received by December 15, 2026. Applications received after that date may be considered until the position is filled. It is anticipated the appointment will begin in Fall 2027. For questions regarding the application process or other inquiries, please contact Mr. Mateo Andres ( chenfacultyservices@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=180698&JobId=184052": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (TAMSCI)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (TAMSCI) Psychological and Brain Sciences Location College Station, TX Open Date 4/17/2026 Position ID 180698 Description The Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences in the College of Arts and Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applications for one full-time, tenure-track assistant professor position with a 9-month academic appointment with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. We are interested in recruiting talented researchers in the field of spinal cord injury research. The successful candidate will bring a robust research program that couples in vivo behavioral/physiological work with cutting-edge tools in neuroscience. The successful candidate will complement the department's existing strengths in topic areas such as neural plasticity, imaging, neural-engineering applications, psychological adjustment, and rehabilitation. This position represents the second phase of a growth initiative in the area supported by TIRR Foundation to increase the breadth and impact of spinal cord injury research at Texas A&M University. The successful candidate will join a rich community of spinal cord injury research in the Texas A&M Spinal Cord Initiative (TAMSCI): a group of nine research labs spanning basic and translational to veterinary and human clinical research. TAMSCI is affiliated with Mission Connect, a collaborative Texas neurotrauma research consortium of scientists from over a dozen Texas research institutions and the Texas Medical Center. Applicants should have an outstanding record of research achievement, demonstrated potential for extramural research funding, and a strong commitment to undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring in psychology and neuroscience. The successful candidate will engage in research, grant applications, and funded projects; teach courses in our graduate and undergraduate programs; and act as an engaged citizen of the department, college, university, and their field. We seek candidates who are well-positioned to publish in top-tier, peer-reviewed journals in their fields and to obtain grants from federal agencies and/or private foundations. Typical teaching loads for the department are 3 courses per year, distributed across undergraduate and graduate programs; evidence of teaching effectiveness is a plus. The Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences at Texas A&M University is a community of scholars committed to generating scientific discoveries in the discipline, providing rigorous and inspiring undergraduate and graduate education, and engaging in outreach about psychology and the application of psychological sciences. We value collaboration, with many faculty having grants and research projects with colleagues inside and beyond the department. The Department has numerous programs to support early career faculty, including a formal mentoring program. Many faculty in the department are also affiliated with the interdisciplinary Institute for Neuroscience (TAMIN). We offer doctoral programs in clinical psychology, cognition and cognitive neuroscience, social and personality psychology, industrial/organizational psychology, and behavioral and cellular neuroscience, and a Master's program in industrial/organizational psychology. We also offer a BS-Behavioral and Cognitive Neurosciece, as well and a BA and a BS in Psychology. We currently have 50 full-time faculty, over 100 PhD students, and approximately 2,000 undergraduate majors. Texas A&M University is a land, sea, and space grant institution that holds the distinction of classification as an R1 Doctoral University (Highest Research Activity), and faculty benefit from the resources and support associated with this designation. Our department is committed to broadening participation in higher education, and has a policy of being responsive to the needs of dual-career couples. The Department is interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching and/or service, will contribute to the breadth and excellence of the academic community, as well as the educational needs of the population of Texas and the global community. Bryan-College Station, home to Texas A&M University, is a vibrant, dynamic, and rapidly growing community that offers cultural diversity, arts, and entertainment, job opportunities, and overall improved quality of life. Located in the heart of the Houston-Dallas-Austin triangle, the region offers the modern amenities of a big city with a warm, small-town charm, and the community's low cost of living is advantageous to the student and general populations, making it an ideal place to live. Texas A&M University is committed to enriching the learning and working environments for all visitors, students, faculty and staff. Qualifications Ph.D in psychology, neuroscience or related field is required. Preference will be given to candidates with demonstrated potential for publishing high quality research in leading journals, writing and obtaining grants, and engaging in high quality teaching and mentoring activities. Application Instructions Applicants must submit: a cover letter, curriculum vitae, personal statement to include philosophy and plans for teaching, research, and service (as applicable), and contact information for at least three references . Application materials must be submitted through Interfolio at: apply.interfolio.com/184052 Priority will be given to applications received by August 1, 2026 , but applications will be accepted until the position is filled. If you have any questions about this search, please direct emails to Dr. Justin Moscarello, Chair of the search committee, at jmm31@tamu.edu . For questions related to the application submission or for additional assistance, please contact Jennifer Fraustro, at jenfrosty12@tamu.edu . *This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants for this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=178645&JobId=182192": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor for Ruminant Health Research",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor for Ruminant Health Research Veterinary Pathobiology Location Canyon, Texas Open Date 3/24/2026 Position ID 178645 Description The College of Veterinary Medicine & Biomedical Sciences (VMBS) at Texas A&M University (TAMU) and Texas A&M AgriLife Research are seeking qualified applicants for one (1) full-time, 11-month academic appointment at the rank of tenure-track, assistant professor in ruminant health research. This position has an expected start date January 1, 2027. The primary goal of this position is to strengthen food‑animal production by conducting applied research in ruminant health relevant to the beef and dairy industries of the Texas High Plains. Priority health issues may include bovine respiratory disease in beef and dairy cattle or mastitis and metritis in dairy systems; emerging diseases in either beef and dairy cattle; digestive disorders; interactions between the rumen and lung microbiomes; antimicrobial use in production systems; transboundary ruminant parasites and other pathogens in food animals; and other conditions affecting ruminant health and productivity. The individual selected for this position will join a successful team of colleagues working within the Veterinary Education, Research and Outreach (VERO) Program ( https://vetmed.tamu.edu/vero ), an exciting collaborative initiative in the heart of a highly productive animal agricultural region. To advance the future of agriculture and food‑animal health in the region, The Texas A&M University System, including Texas A&M University and West Texas A&M University (WTAMU), has invested significantly in new facilities in Canyon, Texas. These include the VERO building, WTAMU’s Agricultural Sciences Complex, and the Texas A&M Veterinary Medical Diagnostic Laboratory. The campus is further strengthened by the newly opened Texas A&M AgriLife High Plains Research and Extension Center, featuring state‑of‑the‑art laboratories and modern research spaces. The successful applicant will be a key member of the growing VERO team, working with other university leaders, agency staff and faculty, and industry partners in guiding the development and growth of this novel initiative. The successful applicant will have access to cattle-feeding research facilities at Nance Ranch (WTAMU) and at Bushland, TX (Texas A&M AgriLife Research/USDA-Agricultural Research Service). The successful applicant will report jointly to the Head of the Department of Veterinary Pathobiology, Texas A&M University, and the Center Director, Texas A&M AgriLife Research; therefore, annual performance reviews will be conducted jointly. Progress toward academic promotion and tenure will be fostered and managed by the Department of Veterinary Pathobiology, Texas A&M University. Expectations: The successful applicant is expected to establish and maintain financial support for research and training of graduate students and post-doctoral fellows in ruminant health management. The candidate will be expected to provide service to the institutional animal care and use committee (IACUC). The successful candidate will also be expected to establish and maintain a USDA approved Hatch project and join at least one USDA Multistate Research projects. The successful candidate will coordinate research activities at shared cattle research facilities with regional institutions as appropriate. Participation in outreach and continuing education as part of the service assignment is encouraged. Appointment: 60-70% of expected effort for this position will be assigned to Research/Scholarship 20-30% to Teaching 10-20% to Academic and Industry Service. Qualifications Required: a PhD degree in a discipline relevant to ruminant animal health and an emerging record of scholarship related to the position focus OR a DVM with specialty training related to ruminant production systems and an emerging record of scholarship related to the position focus Preferred: Candidates with knowledge and experience in ruminant health research/impacts related to beef cattle feedlots, dairy farms, or stocker/cow-calf cattle are encouraged to apply. We especially welcome applications from those candidates with a demonstrated ability to perform independent research, experience in supervising scientific support staff and graduate students, an ability to cooperate and collaborate as an interdisciplinary research team member, the capability to publish in peer-reviewed journals, and the talent and drive to build an independent and extramurally funded and sustainable research program. Candidates with expertise pertinent to North American livestock and food production systems, especially with knowledge and experience in beef and dairy production systems, will be given preference. Application Instructions Applicants must submit the following: • Cover letter describing their interest in the position • Curriculum Vitae • Personal Statement: Your personal statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service as applicable. • Names and contact information (address, telephone number, and e-mail address) of five (5) references. Please submit the application through apply.interfolio.com/182192 . Full consideration will be given to applications received by June 1, 2026, and the search will continue until a suitable candidate is identified. Applicants needing more information are encouraged to contact Dr. Christopher Seabury, chair of the search committee ( cseabury@cvm.tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=191233&JobId=192961": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor Geography of Energy",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor Geography of Energy Geography Location College Station, TX Open Date 9/16/2026 Position ID 191233 Description The Department of Geography in the College of Arts & Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor whose research advances energy and human-environmental geography. We seek applicants who focus on the geographies of energy demand, production, or transitions against the background of changing environmental, economic, or geopolitical forces. This position will support our B.S. degree programs in Geography and Geographic Information Science and Technology, M.S. and Ph.D. degree programs in Geography, the online Master’s of Geoscience program, the B.S. degrees in our college-level Environmental Programs, and a graduate certificate program in Geospatial Intelligence. The successful candidate must have a proven research record and a strong commitment to education and professionalization of undergraduate and graduate students. This is a nine-month full-time annual appointment with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. In addition to research expectations, the successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses and supervise graduate students. Candidates should be dedicated to providing high-quality education for a range of students. Faculty are also expected to participate in appropriate disciplinary service and contribute to departmental and university committees. The Department of Geography ( artsci.tamu.edu/geography ) has thirty faculty members with strengths in biogeography, climatology, geographic information science and technology (GIST), geomorphology, human geography, and human-environment relationships. We are one of eighteen departments in the College of Arts & Sciences ( http://artsci.tamu.edu ). Texas A&M University, a land-, sea-, and space-grant university, is located in a metropolitan area with a dynamic and international community of over a quarter million people. Qualifications Required Qualifications Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Geography or related field at the time of appointment. The successful candidate will have demonstrated potential for excellence in research, with a record of scholarly publications. We also expect that successful candidates will have experience in or demonstrated potential for securing external research funding. Application Instructions Initial review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit a cover letter; a complete CV; a personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service (not to exceed 2 pages in length); and the names and contacts of three references to https://apply.interfolio.com/192961 Informal inquiries or requests for more information may be sent via email to the search committee chair Dr. David Cairns at cairns@tamu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=191929&JobId=193595": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Agriculture and Rural Community Leadership",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Agriculture and Rural Community Leadership Agricultural Leadership, Education, and Communications Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/12/2026 Position ID 191929 Description The Department of Agricultural Leadership, Education, and Communications (ALEC) and College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Texas A&M University invite applications for a full-time, 9-month Assistant Professor faculty position (tenure-track) with academic, scholarly, and faculty accomplishments in Agricultural and Rural Community Leadership. The anticipated start date for this opportunity is January 1, 2028. The successful candidate will contribute to the continued growth of the ALEC academic programs, specifically the Agricultural Leadership (ALED) degree and the M.S. and Ph.D. degree programs in Agricultural Leadership, Education, and Communications. The position carries a Division of Effort of 50% research, 40% teaching, and 10% service. The individual in this position is expected to obtain federal, state, industrial, and/or foundation funding to support and sustain a vigorous research program. Texas is simultaneously one of the most agriculturally productive and most unevenly developed states in the nation. It leads the country in cattle, cotton, hay, sheep, wool, goats, mohair, and horse production and ranks among the top ten producers for more than 40 major commodities. Yet the communities that anchor this agricultural economy face compounding pressures: a rural population of more than three million people who routinely struggle to access healthcare, broadband, education, and economic opportunity that urban counterparts take for granted. At least 74 Texas counties lack a hospital, 20 or more rural hospitals have closed since 2010, and nearly one million rural Texans lack adequate broadband connectivity, costing the state more than $5 billion in lost economic potential annually. This position is designed to advance scholarship at the critical intersection of agriculture and rural community leadership development , recognizing that the strength of Texas’s commodity sectors and the vitality of its rural communities are deeply interdependent. Without capable local leaders navigating disruption, small towns and farm-dependent counties face decline; without thriving rural communities, the infrastructure, labor, and social capital that sustains commodity agriculture erodes. The successful candidate will build a nationally recognized, externally funded research program examining how leaders of Texas’s agricultural commodity industries and rural communities are developed, prepared, and empowered to meet the challenges of 2030 and beyond. Research (50%) The successful candidate will: Develop and lead a vigorous, externally funded research program at the intersection of agriculture and rural community leadership development , with specific relevance to Texas’s agricultural commodity sectors and rural communities. Investigate leadership formation, capacity building, and organizational effectiveness within Texas agricultural commodity industries, such as beef cattle, cotton, corn, sorghum, poultry, dairy, sheep, goat, and specialty crop sectors. Pursue research on rural community leadership challenges facing Texas, such as community capacity building in farm-dependent counties; youth outmigration and succession of rural leadership; civic leadership development in small towns; rural economic diversification and entrepreneurship; and the leadership dimensions of rural health, broadband access, water systems, and infrastructure. Examine the intersections between commodity industry leadership and rural community wellbeing, including the roles of commodity organizations, producer cooperatives, and agricultural associations in sustaining rural communities. Pursue federal, state, industry, and foundation funding to sustain a productive research enterprise, including USDA NIFA, USDA AFRI, USDA Rural Development, Texas Department of Agriculture, Texas A&M AgriLife Research, commodity board partnerships, and private foundations. Produce peer-reviewed scholarship advancing knowledge at the intersection of leadership theory, rural community development, and agricultural systems. Engage directly with commodity leaders, producer organizations, rural community stakeholders, and municipal/county officials to ensure research relevance and promote knowledge transfer. Collaborate with interdisciplinary teams across ALEC, the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, Texas A&M AgriLife Research, Texas A&M AgriLife Extension Service, and partner institutions. Mentor and advise M.S. and Ph.D. students in the ALEC graduate programs. Teaching (40%) The successful candidate will: Teach undergraduate courses in the Agricultural Leadership and Development (ALED) degree program, with particular attention to courses in community leadership, organizational development, and civic engagement, consistent with the candidate’s areas of expertise. Additional coursework could be taught in courses associated with the International Agriculture minor. Develop and teach graduate-level courses in agricultural and rural community leadership for the M.S. and Ph.D. programs in ALEC. Advise and mentor undergraduate students, with particular attention to students enrolled in the ALEC Leadership Honors Program. Contribute curriculum development expertise to evolve academic offerings responsive to the leadership needs of Texas agricultural commodity industries and rural communities. Service (10%) The successful candidate will: Serve on departmental, college, and university committees as appropriate. Engage professionally through national organizations such as the American Association for Agricultural Education (AAAE), the Rural Sociological Society, or comparable entities. Participate in commodity organization advisory boards, industry task forces, rural community leadership programs, and producer leadership initiatives as opportunities arise. Contribute to community outreach through Texas A&M AgriLife Extension Service, with possible engagement with the Texas Rural Leadership Program or similar statewide rural leadership initiatives. The Department The Department of Agricultural Leadership, Education, and Communications is a nationally recognized leader in the social sciences of agriculture and life sciences, housed in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Texas A&M University. ALEC is home to approximately 1,300 undergraduate students, 300 graduate students, and more than 30 faculty scholars. ALEC faculty conduct impactful, interdisciplinary research that addresses critical issues pertaining to food, agriculture, natural resources, youth development, and human health, working where agriculture and the life sciences meet to strengthen both urban and rural communities in Texas, the nation, and worldwide. ALEC offers undergraduate degrees in Agricultural Leadership and Development (ALED) and Agricultural Education (AGED), master’s and doctoral programs in Agricultural Leadership, Education, and Communications, and graduate certificates in Leadership Education Theory and Practice, Extension Education, International Agriculture, and Agriculture eLearning Development. A dedicated stream of ALEC research aims to improve the health, wellbeing, and livelihood of people living in rural areas, supporting initiatives, education, and programming to reinvigorate small towns, identify economic opportunities, and empower local citizens to lead and navigate change. Qualifications Required Qualifications An earned doctorate (Ph.D.) in Agricultural Leadership, Leadership Studies, Agricultural Education, Agricultural and Extension Education, Rural Sociology, Community Development, Psychology, Organizational Leadership, Rural Studies, Human Geography, Anthropology, or a closely related social science discipline from an accredited institution; the degree must be completed by the appointment start date. Demonstrated research focus or scholarly interest in leadership development within agricultural or rural community contexts. Evidence of potential to develop a productive, externally funded research program. Demonstrated potential or record of peer-reviewed publication in refereed journals. Evidence of effective teaching or teaching potential at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Commitment to mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. Evidence of effective oral and written communication skills. A range of methodological expertise essential to the success of a scholar, including statistical analysis (specifically the use of R) required at both ranks Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated ability or potential to develop curriculum, teach, and mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Professional or applied experience in agricultural production, commodity marketing, cooperative management, rural community development, or related fields. An established or emerging research program specifically addressing leadership development for Texas or regional agricultural commodity industries and/or rural communities. Experience working directly with agricultural commodity organizations, producer groups, farm bureaus, rural cooperatives, or rural community organizations. Evidence of strong communication, perspective-taking, and collegiality aligned with effective service at the departmental, campus, and professional levels. Experiences with community-based participatory research, qualitative, quantitative, and mixed-methods research designs relevant to leadership and rural community social science inquiry. Demonstrated commitment to agricultural and rural leadership contexts, with attention to historically underrepresented producers and beginning farmers and ranchers. Knowledge of emerging challenges shaping rural Texas, including broadband equity, rural health access, water systems, infrastructure, and rural workforce development. Familiarity with leadership succession and intergenerational transfer in both farm/ranch operations and rural community governance. A record of, or demonstrated potential for, securing extramural funding from USDA (NIFA, AFRI, Rural Development), state agencies, or commodity and community foundation sources Application Instructions Applicants must submit: A cover letter summarizing interest in and qualifications for the position in .pdf format; A current Curriculum Vitae that includes research activity, teaching experience, and service to the profession in .pdf format; A personal statement that includes a guiding philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service. Limit your statement to 3 pages, Times New Roman, 12 pt font, in .pdf format; and Name and contact information for three professional references. Applications must be submitted online at: apply.interfolio.com/193595 The position will be open until filled. The process of reviewing applications will begin 30 days after the position opens. If you have questions about the position, contact Dr. Summer Odom ( summer.feltonodom@ag.tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=191571&JobId=193273": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Animal Health",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Animal Health Animal Science Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/14/2026 Position ID 191571 Description Assistant Professor in Animal Health Department of Animal Science Texas A&M University POSITION: The Department of Animal Science in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applications for an Assistant Professor in Animal Health. Candidates for Assistant Professor will be tenure-track with demonstrated potential to develop an exemplary teaching, research, and service portfolio. This is a 9-month, full-time appointment with research (60%), teaching (30%), and service (10%) responsibilities and an anticipated start date of August 15, 2027. GENERAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : The primary focus of this position will be to support cutting-edge research and teaching to advance animal health and educate future disciplinary leaders. The successful candidate will enhance a multidisciplinary faculty in animal science. Developing and leading an extramurally funded, internationally recognized research program in animal health will be expected. An individual's emphasis area of research may align with the successful candidate's strengths (ex: immunology, physiology, virology, etc). The successful candidate will contribute to the educational mission of the Department of Animal Science through teaching activities that enhance undergraduate and graduate curricula as well as engage disciplinary stakeholders at the state, national, and international levels. All faculty members in these appointments are required to make significant contributions in scholarly research, teaching, and service. Granting tenure is a recognition of excellence in all three areas. RESOURCES: Texas A&M University is one of only 24 institutions in the nation to hold the triple designation as a land-grant, sea-grant, and space-grant university with several high-quality academic units conducting research, teaching, and service activities in animal science. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive salary, startup package, laboratory space with shared equipment, and access to facilities and core laboratories operated by the Department of Animal Science, Texas A&M AgriLife Research, and the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences. Qualifications REQUIREMENTS : A Ph.D. in Animal Science or a related field emphasizing food microbiology with a research record emphasizing meat microbiology. A demonstrated ability or potential for excellence in research, successful grantsmanship, and contributing to the undergraduate and graduate programs through teaching and mentoring is required. Application Instructions APPLICATION PROCESS: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a cover letter; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service, as applicable; and 4) contact information for five individuals who can provide a critical evaluation of the applicant’s qualifications for the position. Applications will only be accepted through Interfolio (link below): Interfolio Link - https://apply.interfolio.com/193273 Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. For questions, contact Dr. Jerrad Legako, Search Committee Chair, Department of Animal Science, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX 77843-2471, through Ms. Amy Carwile, email: ( amy.carwile@ag.tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193582&JobId=195058": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Artificial Intelligence and Animation",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Artificial Intelligence and Animation College of Performance, Visualization, & Fine Arts (Department) Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/13/2026 Position ID 193582 Description The Texas A&M University College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts (PVFA) and the Virtual Production Institute (VPI) invite applications for a full-time, 9-month tenure-track faculty position in Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Animation at the rank of Assistant Professor within the Technical Arts and Interactive Media section . The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. Candidates should demonstrate strong potential for scholarly achievement and the ability to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research and/or creative works agenda in AI and Animation. We welcome applicants working broadly across animation, computer graphics, artificial intelligence, character animation, human motion, and related areas. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research and/or creative works can advance performance capture and animation, broadly understood to include the capture, analysis, synthesis, modeling, and generation of expressive human and character movement. Relevant approaches may include motion capture, markerless and multimodal capture, AI-driven and generative motion models, computational performance, expressive character animation, real-time animation, and related emerging techniques. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: Animation craft and aesthetics, including performance, acting, timing, and character in animated movement Character animation and advanced animation techniques AI-driven animation, generative motion models, and neural representations Human motion analysis, synthesis, and computational performance Performance and motion capture in complex and real-time environments Markerless and multimodal capture approaches Expressive character movement and digital performance Applications in media and entertainment, virtual production, game-engine-based character performance, simulation and training, and other interdisciplinary domains The successful candidate will be expected to establish an active program of research and/or creative works, pursue external funding opportunities, disseminate work through publications, presentations, exhibitions, software systems, patents, or other recognized scholarly outcomes, and contribute to the national and international visibility of PVFA and the VPI. The successful candidate will also be expected to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, advise and mentor graduate students, participate in all aspects of PVFA's and the VPI's activities, and serve the professional community. About the College The College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts was formed in 2022 at Texas A&M University, bringing together the Department of Visualization, the Department of Performance Studies, and the Dance Science Program. PVFA is built without departments. Faculty sections and academic programs sit at the college level, which places musicians, composers, actors, directors, choreographers, dancers, painters, cinematographers, technical artists, level designers, spatial audio engineers, tools programmers, ethnomusicologists, art historians, UI/UX designers, and virtual set designers in one structure rather than in separate units. The College places a heavy emphasis on collaboration and interdisciplinary work in both scholarly and creative research. The College offers the BS, MS, and MFA in Visualization, a PhD in Visual Computing and Interactive Media, and degree programs in music, dance, and theatre. It operates at the main campus in Bryan-College Station and as part of Texas A&M – Fort Worth, with facilities equipped for work at the intersection of the arts and emerging technology. Applicants should consult the college's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/ ) to review our academic and research programs. About the Virtual Production Institute This position is aligned with the mission of the Virtual Production Institute. Formed in 2023, the VPI is a technology-focused ecosystem for teaching, learning, and research. It develops emerging applications and the skilled workforce needed for virtual reality, augmented reality, sensors, projectors, and computing across multiple disciplines and industries. Its facilities in Bryan-College Station and Fort Worth include a performance capture and sound stage with supporting technology studio classrooms. Applicants should consult the institute's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/institutes/vpi/ ). About the Ph.D. in Visual Computing and Interactive Media (VCIM) The Ph.D. in Visual Computing and Interactive Media (VCIM) provides an interdisciplinary environment for advanced research at the intersection of computing, visualization, interactive media, and the arts. The program brings together faculty and students working across areas such as computer graphics, animation, artificial intelligence, visualization, virtual and augmented reality, interactive systems, and related creative and computational disciplines. The successful candidate will have opportunities to advise VCIM doctoral students, develop graduate courses related to their research expertise , and collaborate across the College and the University on interdisciplinary research and creative projects. The successful candidate is expected to participate actively in the VCIM doctoral program, including advising Ph.D. students and, when eligible, chairing dissertation committees. Qualifications Required: A Ph.D. in Computer Science, Visualization, Visual Computing, Computer Graphics, Animation, or a closely related field is required. The successful candidate is expected to play an active role in the Ph.D. in Visual Computing and Interactive Media (VCIM) program, including advising doctoral students and, when eligible, chairing dissertation committees. Strong written and verbal communication skills are required , as is an enthusiasm for exploring concepts, solving problems, and collaborating across the range of artistic, technical, and humanistic disciplines represented in the College. Candidates should have a demonstrated record of scholarly research and/or creative activity, participation in multidisciplinary research and/or creative activities, university teaching experience, student mentorship, and academic service. Evidence of, or potential for, extramural funding and industry partnerships is desirable. Application Instructions Interested individuals must apply through Interfolio : apply.interfolio.com/195058 and submit the following materials (embed links to your work, student work, etc. where appropriate) : Cover letter Curriculum vitae (embed links to your work) Personal Statement: Your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service. Portfolio of creative work (link to a website preferred) Names and contact information of five (5) professional references Review of applications will begin 30 days after posting and will continue until the position is filled. Questions should be directed to Ergun Akleman, Chair of Search Committee (email: ergun.akleman@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=191600&JobId=193297": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Dairy Cattle Management",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Dairy Cattle Management Animal Science Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/14/2026 Position ID 191600 Description Assistant Professor in Dairy Cattle Management Department of Animal Science Texas A&M University POSITION: The Department of Animal Science in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applications for an Assistant Professor in Dairy Cattle Management. Candidates for Assistant Professor will be tenure-track with demonstrated potential to develop an exemplary teaching, research, and service portfolio. This is a 9-month, full-time appointment with research (50%), teaching (40%), and service (10%) responsibilities and an anticipated start date of August 15, 2027. GENERAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: The primary focus of this position will be to support cutting-edge research and teaching to advance dairy science and educate future disciplinary leaders. The successful candidate will enhance a multidisciplinary faculty in animal science. The successful candidate will contribute to the educational mission of the Department of Animal Science through teaching activities that enhance undergraduate and graduate curricula as well as engage disciplinary stakeholders at the state, national, and international levels, specifically via the U.S. Dairy Education & Training Consortium (USDETC). Developing and leading an extramurally funded, internationally recognized research program in dairy science will be expected. All faculty members in these appointments are required to make significant contributions in scholarly research, teaching, and service. An individual's emphasis area of research may align with the successful candidate's strengths (ex: precision management, sensor technology, nutrition, etc). RESOURCES: Texas A&M University is one of only 24 institutions in the nation to hold the triple designation as a land-grant, sea-grant, and space-grant university with several high-quality academic units conducting research, teaching, and service activities in animal science. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive salary, startup package, laboratory space with shared equipment, and access to facilities and core laboratories operated by the Department of Animal Science, Texas A&M AgriLife Research, and the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences. Qualifications REQUIREMENTS : A Ph.D. in Animal Science or a related field emphasizing dairy science. A demonstrated ability or potential for excellence in research, successful grantsmanship, and contributing to the undergraduate and graduate programs through teaching and mentoring is required. Application Instructions APPLICATION PROCESS: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a cover letter; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service, as applicable; and 4) contact information for five individuals who can provide a critical evaluation of the applicant’s qualifications for the position. Applications will only be accepted through Interfolio (link below): Interfolio Link - https://apply.interfolio.com/193297 Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. For questions, contact Dr. Sushil Paudyal, Search Committee Chair, Department of Animal Science, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX 77843-2471, through Ms. Amy Carwile, email: ( amy.carwile@ag.tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=188435&JobId=190376": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Fungal Biology of Plant-Associated Systems",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Fungal Biology of Plant-Associated Systems Plant Pathology and Microbiology Location College Station Open Date 8/11/2026 Position ID 188435 Description The Department of Plant Pathology and Microbiology in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences on the main College Station campus of Texas A&M University invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor with research (60%), teaching (30%), and service (10%) responsibilities. The expected start date is August 15, 2027. Applicants will be considered for the faculty title of Assistant Professor in the Department of Plant Pathology and Microbiology ( https://plantpathology.tamu.edu/ ). The successful candidate will be offered a competitive salary, startup package, and laboratory space in the Plant Pathology and Microbiology building. These facilities include state-of-the-art growth chambers and greenhouses, together with core facilities for Microscopy and Imaging, Genomics and Sequencing, High-Performance Research Computing, the Center for Structural Biology, the Biomolecular NMR Laboratory, and the Protein Chemistry Laboratory. Texas A&M University has historically had a strength in fungal biology. This position is aimed at continuing this excellence. GENERAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: The successful candidate is expected to develop and lead an independent, high-impact, extramurally funded, and internationally recognized research program in fungal biology of plant and plant-associated systems. Candidates studying fungi as pathogens, mutualists, endophytes, saprotrophs, or members of the broader plant and soil mycobiome are encouraged to apply, provided the work connects to plant health or plant processes. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, fungal pathogenesis and the molecular basis of fungal-plant interactions; plant immune and physiological responses to fungi and the mechanisms governing disease, resistance, or symbiosis; the structure, function, and assembly of plant, rhizosphere, and soil mycobiomes; population, evolutionary, and comparative genomics of fungi and their hosts; fundamental fungal biology, including genetics, development, physiology, cell biology, and metabolism; fungal biotechnology within the scope of fungal–plant interactions, including natural products, enzymes, synthetic biology, and biological control or biostimulant applications; and detection, diagnostics, and surveillance of fungi relevant to plant and agricultural systems. Research on soil-associated fungi is encouraged where it intersects with plant health or plant processes, such as the rhizosphere, nutrient acquisition, plant–microbe feedbacks, or microbiome-mediated plant performance. Responsibilities will also include teaching graduate and undergraduate courses in the candidate’s area of expertise which may include mycology, fungal biology, plant pathology, plant-microbe interactions, or microbiology, chairing and serving on graduate committees. Candidates are also expected to provide service to the department, university, and scientific community. Opportunities to collaborate with Texas A&M AgriLife Research and Texas A&M AgriLife Extension are available and encouraged. The Department of Plant Pathology and Microbiology hosts an undergraduate program in Bioenvironmental Sciences and graduate programs in Plant Pathology. RESOURCES: Texas A&M University is a public, land-grant institution with many high-quality academic units conducting research across agriculture and the life sciences, and it promotes multiscale, interdisciplinary research spanning foundational knowledge and application. In addition to departments, the university hosts interdisciplinary graduate programs including Ecology and Evolutionary Biology, Genetics and Genomics, and Molecular and Environmental Plant Sciences. The campus offers excellent core facilities ( https://vpr.tamu.edu/research-support/core-facilities/ ) supporting protein and nucleic-acid technologies, high- and super-resolution fluorescence imaging, high-performance computing, and plant growth and transformation, among others. Relevant centers and cores include the AgriLife Genomics and Bioinformatics Core Facility, the High-throughput Automated Plant Genotyping Greenhouses, the Experimental Genomics Core Facility, the High-Performance Research Computing facility, the Microscopy and Imaging Center, and the Integrated Metabolomics Analysis Core. ABOUT THE COMMUNITY: The University is in College Station, which together with the twin city of Bryan forms a metropolitan community of approximately 275,000 people with high-quality amenities and a low cost of living. In addition to excellent health, education, and recreational services, the community offers a rich variety of cultural activities typical of a major university environment, including sports, museums, music, art, and theater. The College Station–Bryan area is also centrally located among Texas’ major metropolitan areas, including Houston, Dallas–Fort Worth, Austin, and San Antonio. Qualifications A Ph.D. or equivalent degree in plant pathology, mycology, microbiology, or a related area including but not limited to molecular biology, genetics, genomics, cell biology, ecology, evolutionary biology, or computational biology is required by the time of appointment. Candidates with postdoctoral research experience are preferred. Evidence of the potential to develop an outstanding, extramurally funded research program and indications of strong mentorship and teaching skills are required. Prior experience with grantsmanship, teaching, and mentorship is desirable. Application Instructions Applicants must submit application through Interfolio, which will serve as the employment application. In addition, (1) a cover letter with a statement of the applicant’s interest and suitability for the position; (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae; (3) a three-page personal statement that includes philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service, and (4) contact information for three individuals who can provide a critical evaluation of the applicant’s qualifications. Applications will be accepted at apply.interfolio.com/190376 . Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Questions may be addressed to the search committee chair Dr. Sanjay Antony-Babu, Department of Plant Pathology and Microbiology, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX 77843 (sanjay@tamu.edu). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=191208&JobId=192972": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Materials Science and Engineering – Quantum Materials",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Materials Science and Engineering – Quantum Materials Materials Science & Engineering Location College Station Texas Open Date 9/4/2026 Position ID 191208 Description The Department of Materials Science and Engineering (MSEN) in the College of Engineering at Texas A&M University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the area of Quantum Materials. We seek candidates who will establish and lead internationally recognized, externally funded research programs in the broad area of quantum materials, with a demonstrated emphasis on experimental synthesis/growth and characterization of emergent quantum materials. This position is part of a university-wide cluster hire in Quantum Sciences and Technology at Texas A&M University, for a total of 5 faculty positions across engineering and science, designed to strengthen a highly interdisciplinary community at the intersection of physics, chemistry, materials science, and electrical engineering. The initiative seeks faculty who will advance fundamental understanding and technological applications of quantum materials through collaborative research spanning synthesis, characterization, theory, computation, and device development. Successful candidates will engage with a broad network of researchers across departments and institutes at Texas A&M, fostering new approaches to quantum phenomena and enabling next-generation quantum technologies. Areas of particular interest include, but are not limited to: Experimental synthesis and growth for quantum materials and related systems; Emergent properties and phenomena of quantum materials; Low-dimensional and correlated quantum materials, with applications in twistronics, spintronics, and devices; Defects and solid-state qubits for quantum sensing and other emerging technologies; Topological phases and quantum geometry, with applications in quantum information science and device applications. The successful applicant will be required to teach; advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students; develop an independent, externally funded research program; participate in all aspects of the Department’s activities; and serve the profession. Through effective collaboration across the discipline of engineering, the MSEN department has numerous opportunities for the development of laboratories and sponsorship of research activities. MSEN is a relatively new and growing department, currently with 22 full time and 6 joint tenured/tenure-track faculty. The student enrollment has continuously grown since the establishment of the department, and currently we have about 300 undergraduate students (sophomore, junior, and seniors), and about 200 graduate students. MSEN is an integral part of the TAMU College of Engineering, with excellent interaction with faculty in other engineering programs to support large-scale college initiatives and access to graduate students to assist in research activities. Strong written and verbal communication skills are required. Applicants should consult the Department’s website to review our academic and research programs ( engineering.tamu.edu/materials ). Qualifications Applicants must have an earned doctorate in Materials Science and Engineering or a closely related science or engineering discipline at the time of appointment. Demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching, research, and service is required. Postdoctoral research experience or equivalent is highly desirable, but is not required. Application Instructions Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a personal statement (include plans for research, teaching, and service, as applicable), and a list of four contact references (include phone numbers, postal addresses, and email addresses) by applying for this specific position at https://apply.interfolio.com/192972. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Full consideration will be given to applications received by December 1, 2026. Applications received after that date may be considered until the position is filled. It is anticipated that the appointment will begin August 1, 2027. For questions regarding the application process or other inquiries, please contact Mr. Kyle Broxon at kbroxon@tamu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=189286&JobId=191142": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Applied Microeconomics or Macroeconomics (Energy Economics)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Applied Microeconomics or Macroeconomics (Energy Economics) Economics Location College Station, TX, USA Open Date 9/1/2026 Position ID 189286 Description The Department of Economics in the College of Arts & Sciences at Texas A&M University is seeking to fill one position at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor in applied microeconomics or macroeconomics, with a substantive focus on energy economics. We welcome candidates who study energy-related questions using tools from applied microeconomics, macroeconomics, industrial organization, public economics, labor economics, or related fields. This is a full-time, 9-month academic appointment with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. In addition to publishing in leading journals, the successful candidate is expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, contribute to the Department's intellectual life, and serve on appropriate departmental committees. Qualifications This position requires a Ph.D. in economics or a related field. Successful candidates must demonstrate excellence in research and teaching. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted online at https://apply.interfolio.com/191142 . All applicants must submit a cover letter, vita, personal statement to include philosophy and plans for teaching, research, and service as applicable, and arrange for three confidential reference letters to be submitted online. Applicants will be asked to submit the name and email for three confidential letters of reference to be submitted; referees will receive an automated notification from Interfolio to upload their letter to the website. Applications for both new Ph.D.s and those with tenure-track experience are due by November 1, 2026, for fullest consideration. Applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to Chelsi Bass . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193558&JobId=195034": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Art & Design in Intelligent Systems and Computational Design",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Art & Design in Intelligent Systems and Computational Design College of Performance, Visualization, & Fine Arts (Department) Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/8/2026 Position ID 193558 Description The Texas A&M University College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts (PVFA) invites applications for a tenure-track, full-time, 9-month Assistant Professor of Art & Design in Intelligent Systems and Computational Design position in the Art & Design (ARTD) Section of the Visualization Program . The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. The ARTD Section seeks an artist and/or designer whose work advances contemporary creative practice by blending intelligent systems, computational design, and generative methodologies as primary modes of artistic and design inquiry . We are particularly interested in candidates whose creative and scholarly work explores generative systems, machine learning, creative coding, computational creativity, and emerging forms of AI-enabled artistic and design production. We seek candidates whose work investigates the creative, cultural, aesthetic, and critical possibilities of machine learning, generative AI, algorithmic systems, computational visualization, procedural design, and related computational approaches to art and design. The Section welcomes research and creative practice across areas including: Artificial Intelligence and Creative Practice Creative applications of intelligent systems and machine learning, human-AI collaboration, computational creativity, and emerging approaches to artistic and design production. Computational Design Algorithmic design, creative coding, procedural systems, computational visualization, generative workflows, and computational methods for design innovation. Critical and Experimental Futures Research and creative work, including publications, that examines the cultural, social, ethical, and aesthetic implications of AI and computational technologies. Across these areas, we are interested in how computation functions not simply as a tool, but as a medium for artistic inquiry, design research, experimentation, and creative production. We are particularly interested in candidates who demonstrate how intelligent systems, generative methodologies, and computational design function as conceptual, aesthetic, and material frameworks for contemporary artistic and design practice. Computational and AI-driven practices may result in a wide range of outcomes, including screen-based experiences, physical artifacts, fabricated objects, spatial installations, immersive environments, performances, and hybrid digital-physical works. Physical making and material experimentation are welcomed as potential manifestations of computational practice but are neither required nor privileged over other forms of creative output. Consistent with the interdisciplinary mission of the College, we value candidates who demonstrate the ability to collaborate across art, design, visualization, computing, engineering, architecture, the humanities, performance, and the performing arts to produce meaningful creative, scholarly, and public-facing work. The successful candidate will develop and maintain an active, externally recognized program of creative practice and/or research; contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in intelligent systems, computational design, generative creative practice, and contemporary art and design methodologies ; mentor undergraduate and graduate students; develop innovative curriculum; and contribute to academic service. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses aligned with their areas of expertise, which may include artificial intelligence for creative practice, generative design, computational design, creative coding, computational visualization, digital fabrication, digital sculpture, and studio-based creative practice; advise and mentor MFA students; contribute to the continued evolution of graduate studio-based research and creative practice within the Visualization Program; participate in collaborative and interdisciplinary activities; and serve the profession, the program, and the university community. About the College The College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts was formed in 2022 at Texas A&M University, bringing together the Department of Visualization, the Department of Performance Studies, and the Dance Science Program. PVFA is built without departments. Faculty sections and academic programs sit at the college level, which places musicians, composers, actors, directors, choreographers, dancers, painters, cinematographers, technical artists, level designers, spatial audio engineers, tools programmers, ethnomusicologists, art historians, UI/UX designers, and virtual set designers in one structure rather than in separate units. The College places a heavy emphasis on collaboration and interdisciplinary work in both scholarly and creative research. The College offers the BS, MS, and MFA in Visualization, a PhD in Visual Computing and Interactive Media, and degree programs in music, dance, and theatre. It operates at the main campus in Bryan-College Station and as part of Texas A&M – Fort Worth, with facilities equipped for work at the intersection of the arts and emerging technology. Applicants should consult the college's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/ ) to review our academic and research programs. Qualifications Candidates must hold a terminal degree (MFA, MDes , PhD, or equivalent) in Computational Design, Digital Media, Media Arts, Creative Technologies, Human-Computer Interaction, or a closely related field. Candidates who are ABD will be considered, provided they demonstrate clear progress toward completion. Candidates must demonstrate an active creative and/or scholarly practice in computational design, intelligent systems, generative methodologies, creative coding, computational visualization, artificial intelligence, or related approaches to contemporary art and design . We seek evidence of creative, scholarly, and/or professional accomplishment appropriate to career stage and a demonstrated capacity to contribute to undergraduate and graduate education. Preference will be given to applicants with: A record of exhibitions, publications, presentations, creative works, funded projects, or other forms of disciplinary dissemination. Experience teaching artificial intelligence, computational design, creative coding, generative design, visualization, or related subjects at the university level. Evidence of interdisciplinary collaboration across art, design, computing, engineering, architecture, performance, or related fields. Engagement with critical, theoretical, ethical, cultural, or aesthetic questions surrounding artificial intelligence and computational technologies. Experience developing innovative approaches to computational making, generative systems, computational visualization, AI-enabled creative practice, or hybrid digital-physical production. Demonstrated potential to establish regional, national, or international recognition through creative and/or scholarly work. Application Instructions Applicants must submit the following materials through Interfolio : apply.interfolio.com/195034 Cover letter Curriculum vitae (embed links to your work/student work you mentor) Portfolio of creative work (link to a website preferred) Personal Statement: Your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service as applicable. Names and contact information of five professional references Review of applications will begin 30 days after posting and will continue until the position is filled. Questions regarding this position may be directed to Hollis Hammonds, Chair of the Search Commi ttee ( hollis_hammonds@tamu.edu ) . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=186899&JobId=189122": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Biological Engineering",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Biological Engineering Biological and Agricultural Engineering Location College Station, Texas Open Date 8/1/2026 Position ID 186899 Description The Department of Biological and Agricultural Engineering in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applications for a 9-month, full-time, tenure-track faculty position in Biological Engineering The successful candidate will be expected to: 1) use a multi-disciplinary systems approach to develop a nationally recognized research program focusing on but not limited to the following areas: (a) application of engineering principles and tools to biological systems - including microbes, viruses, toxins, plants, and animals - to optimize agricultural production, food safety, and environmental safety and sustainability, (b) analysis, sensing and modeling (computational/numerical, molecular) of microbial and organic materials including micro,- nanoplastics in food and airborne pathogen control for a deep understanding of the role of food processing technologies on microbial behavior and product safety; (c) design of processes and technologies for food, bioprocess, and molecular engineering applications, including food manufacturing systems, bioprocess development, and molecular-level engineering of biological components, with emphasis on structure–function relationships in biomolecules and complex biological systems, as well as food quality, safety, functionality, and sustainability, including management of bioaerosol emission, waste valorization and circular bioeconomy approaches; 2) develop and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in engineering based on the interests and needs of the Biological and Agricultural Engineering Department; 3) secure extramural funding from a variety of sources including federal, state, industry and private sources; and 4) serve the Department, College and University as assigned, and as opportunity and expertise permits. The candidate should aspire to lead a research program that fuses molecular/cellular bioscience and molecular modeling with quantitative engineering analysis/synthesis approaches, including Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning. Areas of high priority include cutting edge, multiscale, data-driven approaches in non-medical molecular biotechnology, AI-enabled digital representations of biological systems, microbiome engineering (with emphasis on designing novel microbial capabilities to enable circular bio-economy, foster sustainable agriculture, and mitigate the impacts of climate change), and food safety, including applications in materials, chemicals, agriculture, sustainability, energy and the indoor / outdoor environment. Collaboration with AgriLife Extension Faculty, participation in professional development activities and an appropriate level of service to the department, institution, and/or profession is expected. Qualifications Required Qualifications: Applicants must have an earned doctorate (Ph.D.) in Biological Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Agricultural Engineering, Food Engineering, or closely related engineering discipline. The ideal candidate will have a strong commitment to lead a novel research program in the engineering of biological based products, their processes and mitigation of contaminants using experimental and modeling tools. Successful candidates must have excellent verbal and written communication skills, an ability to work both independently and in multi-disciplinary teams and a willingness to advise and mentor students. Applicants must be licensed or be eligible to obtain a license as a Professional Engineer at the time of appointment. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated potential to excel in conducting and publishing high impact research, developing an independent research program, collaborating with interdisciplinary teams of engineers and scientists and successful grant writing. Motivation and skills to educate students through academic programs in engineering at BAEN including the Agricultural Systems Management (AGSM) curriculum and in other departments. Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply through the Texas A&M University faculty job board hosted by Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/189122 and upload the following: 1) cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) a three-page personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching and service, 4) names and contact information of three (3) professional references. Please direct any questions to Dr. Maria King at Maria.King@ag.tamu.edu . Review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=189282&JobId=191137": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Health Economics",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Health Economics Economics Location College Station, TX, USA Open Date 9/1/2026 Position ID 189282 Description The Department of Economics in the College of Arts & Sciences at Texas A&M University is seeking to fill one position at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor in the field of Economics of Health / Health Economics. This position is a 9-month full-time academic appointment with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. In addition to publishing in leading journals, the successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses and supervise graduate students as well as participate in the applied microeconomics workshop, serve on an appropriate department committee, and be involved as a member of Ph.D. dissertation committees. All appointees are expected to be committed to teaching and research of the highest quality. Qualifications This position requires a Ph.D. in economics or a related field. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted online at https://apply.interfolio.com/191137 . Applicants should submit a cover letter, vita, a personal statement to include philosophy and plans on research, teaching, and service as appropriate, and arrange for three letters of reference to be submitted online. Applicants will be asked to submit the name and email for three confidential letters of reference to be submitted; referees will receive an automated notification from Interfolio to upload their letter to the website. For fullest consideration, applications should be received by November 1, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to Chelsi Bass . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193570&JobId=195047": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Interactive Spatial Audio and Multimodal Computing",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Interactive Spatial Audio and Multimodal Computing College of Performance, Visualization, & Fine Arts (Department) Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/12/2026 Position ID 193570 Description The Texas A&M University College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts (PVFA) and the Virtual Production Institute (VPI) invite applications for a full-time, 9-month tenure-track position in Interactive Spatial Audio and Multimodal Computing at the rank of Assistant Pr ofessor in the Visual Computing and Computational Media (VCCM) section. The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. Candidates should demonstrate a strong potential for scholarly achievement and an ability to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research agenda. This position sits at the intersection of AI, multimodal computing, and audio and media technologies, with opportunities for research spanning computing, perception , and immersive systems. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: 3D and Spatial Audio Rendering and Computational Acoustics Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Audio and Multimodal Computing Visual Computing and Multimodal Perception for Media and Entertainment, Simulation and Training, and/or Health and Wellness Interactive Audio Systems and Immersive Media for VR/AR/XR and Virtual Production Perceptual Evaluation and Psychoacoustics Hardware and Software Integration for Interactive and Multimodal Systems Audio Capture, Processing, and Sensing Audio Modeling and Synthesis ; Multimodal Content Generation The successful candidate will be expected to establish an active program of research , pursue external funding opportunities, disseminate work through publications, presentations, software systems, patents, or other recognized scholarly outcomes, and contribute to the national and international visibility of PVFA and the VPI. The s uccessful candidate will also be expected to teach course s at the undergraduate and graduate levels , advise and m entor graduate students , including the Ph.D. in Visual Computing and Interactive Media program , participate in all aspects of PVFA’s and the VPI’s activities, and serve the professional community. About the College The College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts was formed in 2022 at Texas A&M University, bringing together the Department of Visualization, the Department of Performance Studies, and the Dance Science Program. PVFA is built without departments. Faculty sections and academic programs sit at the college level, which places musicians, composers, actors, directors, choreographers, dancers, painters, cinematographers, technical artists, level designers, spatial audio engineers, tools programmers, ethnomusicologists, art historians, UI/UX designers, and virtual set designers in one structure rather than in separate units. The College places a heavy emphasis on collaboration and interdisciplinary work in both scholarly and creative research. The College offers the BS, MS, and MFA in Visualization, a PhD in Visual Computing and Interactive Media, and degree programs in music, dance, and theatre. It operates at the main campus in Bryan-College Station and as part of Texas A&M – Fort Worth, with facilities equipped for work at the intersection of the arts and emerging technology. Applicants should consult the college's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/ ) to review our academic and research programs. About the Virtual Production Institute This position is aligned with the mission of the Virtual Production Institute. Formed in 2023, the VPI is a technology-focused ecosystem for teaching, learning, and research. It develops emerging applications and the skilled workforce needed for virtual reality, augmented reality, sensors, projectors, and computing across multiple disciplines and industries. Its facilities in Bryan-College Station and Fort Worth include a performance capture and sound stage with support ing technology studio classrooms. Applicants should consult the institute's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/institutes/vpi/ ). Qualifications Required: PhD in Computer Science, Electrical Engineering, Media Arts and Technology, Acoustics, or a closely related field. Candidates who are ABD will be considered, provided they demonstrate clear progress forward completion. Demonstrated potential to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research program Evidence of research accomplishment appropriate to career stage Strong written and verbal communication skills Preferred: Potential for extramural funding and industry partnerships University teaching experience and student mentorship Participation in multidisciplinary research Record of academic or professional service Application Instructions Interested individuals must apply through Interfolio : apply.interfolio.com/195047 and submit the following materials (embed links to your work, student work, etc. where appropriate) : Cover letter Curriculum vitae (embed links to your work/student work you mentor) Personal Statement: Your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service. Names and contact information of five (5) professional references Review of applications will begin 30 days after posting and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about this position should be directed to Xin Li, Chair of the Search Committee (email: xinli@tamu.edu ; phone: 979-845-9004 ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=189273&JobId=191129": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of International Macroeconomics",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of International Macroeconomics Economics Location College Station, TX, USA Open Date 9/1/2026 Position ID 189273 Description The Department of Economics in the College of Arts & Sciences at Texas A&M University is seeking to fill one position at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor in the field of International Macroeconomics. This position is a 9-month full-time academic appointment with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. In addition to publishing in leading journals, the successful candidate is expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, contribute to the Department's intellectual life, and serve on appropriate departmental committees. Qualifications This position requires a Ph.D. in economics or a related field. Successful candidates must demonstrate excellence in research and teaching. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted online at http://apply.interfolio.com/191129 . All applicants must submit an application letter, vita, a personal statement to include philosophy and plans for teaching, research and service as applicable, and arrange for three confidential reference letters to be submitted online. Applicants will be asked to submit the name and email for three confidential letters of reference to be submitted; referees will receive an automated notification from Interfolio to upload their letter to the website. Applications for both new Ph.D.s and those with tenure-track experience are due by November 1, 2026, for fullest consideration. Applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to Chelsi Bass . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193578&JobId=195055": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Scenic and Technical Design",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Scenic and Technical Design College of Performance, Visualization, & Fine Arts (Department) Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/8/2026 Position ID 193578 Description The Texas A&M University College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts (PVFA) invite applications for a full-time, 9-month tenure-track position in Scenic and Technical Design at the rank of Assistant Professor in Dance, Music, Theatre, & Performance section. The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. We seek artist-scholars whose work explores how emerging technologies, interdisciplinary collaboration, and innovative modes of performance enhance and expand the experience of live theatre and performance. Candidates should demonstrate a strong potential for scholarly achievement and an ability to establish a nationally and internationally recognized creative works and/or research agenda centered on the creation of interdisciplinary, virtual/hybrid, media-integrated, and devised performance, pursue external funding opportunities, disseminate work through performances, exhibitions, publications, presentations, software systems, patents, or other recognized scholarly outcomes, and contribute to the national and international visibility of PVFA and the VPI. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: Scenic Design & Visual Design for Live Entertainment Digital Integration & Virtual Stage Design Interactive Participatory Stages Remote Hybrid Productions and Mixed Reality Performance Automation, Robotics, and Multimodal Interaction Site-specific Augmented Installations The successful candidate will also be expected to teach courses in stage, scenic, virtual and technical design at the undergraduate level, advise and mentor graduate students, design for the theatre program season, mentor theatrical design students, participate in collaborative activities with the Virtual Production Institute , and serve the professional community . . About the College The College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts was formed in 2022 at Texas A&M University, bringing together the Department of Visualization, the Department of Performance Studies, and the Dance Science Program. PVFA is built without departments. Faculty sections and academic programs sit at the college level, which places musicians, composers, actors, directors, choreographers, dancers, painters, cinematographers, technical artists, level designers, spatial audio engineers, tools programmers, ethnomusicologists, art historians, UI/UX designers, and virtual set designers in one structure rather than in separate units. The College places a heavy emphasis on collaboration and interdisciplinary work in both scholarly and creative research. The College offers the BS, MS, and MFA in Visualization, a PhD in Visual Computing and Interactive Media, and degree programs in music, dance, and theatre. It operates at the main campus in Bryan-College Station and as part of Texas A&M – Fort Worth, with facilities equipped for work at the intersection of the arts and emerging technology. Applicants should consult the college's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/ ) to review our academic and research programs. About the Virtual Production Institute This position is aligned with the mission of the Virtual Production Institute. Formed in 2023, the VPI is a technology-focused ecosystem for teaching, learning, and research. It develops emerging applications and the skilled workforce needed for virtual reality, augmented reality, sensors, projectors, and computing across multiple disciplines and industries. Its facilities in Bryan-College Station and Fort Worth include a performance capture and sound stage with supporting technology studio classrooms. Applicants should consult the institute's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/institutes/vpi/ ). Qualifications Qualifications: Required Terminal degree (MFA, PhD, or equivalent) in Scenic Design, Production Design, Theatre Design, Performance Design, or a related field. Candidates who are ABD will be considered, provided they demonstrate clear progress forward completion. Demonstrated potential for nationally and internationally recognized research and/or creative activity. Expertise in scenic design and live performance environments. Evidence of collaborative and interdisciplinary creative practice. Experience incorporating emerging technologies into performance design or production. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Preferred Research or creative work focused on immersive, virtual, hybrid, media-integrated, or devised performance. Experience with mixed reality, virtual production, interactive performance systems, or digital performance technologies. Record of external funding, industry partnerships, or sponsored creative projects. Experience building interdisciplinary collaborations across the arts, and beyond. Professional recognition through publications, exhibitions, performances, commissions, patents, software systems, or other scholarly and creative outputs. Evidence of successful teaching and student mentorship. Application Instructions Application Instructions Interested individuals must apply through Interfolio : apply.interfolio.com/195055 . Cover letter Curriculum vitae (embed links to your work) Personal Statement: Your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service Portfolio of creative work (link to a website preferred) Names and contact information of five (5) professional references Review of applications will begin 30 days after posting and will continue until the position is filled. Questions should be directed to Dinesh Yadav , Chair of Search Committee (email: yadavd@tamu.edu ) . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=189943&JobId=191770": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Technology and Behavioral/Experimental Economics",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Technology and Behavioral/Experimental Economics Economics Location College Station, TX, USA Open Date 9/1/2026 Position ID 189943 Description The Department of Economics in the College of Arts & Sciences at Texas A&M University is seeking to fill one position at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor in the field of Economics of Technology and Behavioral and Experimental Economics. This position is a 9-month full-time academic appointment with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. In addition to publishing in leading journals, the successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses and supervise graduate students, as well as participate in the behavioral and experimental workshop, serve on an appropriate department committee, and be involved as a member of Ph.D. dissertation committees. Qualifications This position requires a Ph.D. in economics or a related field. All appointees are expected to be committed to teaching and research of the highest quality. Application Instructions Applications must be submitted online at https://apply.interfolio.com/191770 . Applicants must submit a cover letter, vita, a personal statement to include philosophy and plans on research, teaching, and service, and arrange for three confidential letters of reference to be submitted online. For fullest consideration, applications should be received by October 15, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the search may be directed to Chelsi Bass . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193573&JobId=195049": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Theatrical Production Management and Research",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor of Theatrical Production Management and Research College of Performance, Visualization, & Fine Arts (Department) Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/8/2026 Position ID 193573 Description The Texas A&M University College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts (PVFA) invites applications for a full-time, 9-month tenure-track position in Theatrical Production Management and Research at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Dance, Music, and Theatre Performance section. The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. Candidates should demonstrate strong potential for scholarly achievement and an ability to establish a nationally and internationally recognized agenda for research and creative work focused on producing and researching structures that make interdisciplinary, virtual/hybrid, media-integrated, and devised performance possible. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to : Digital Transformation and Technology producing and scheduling systems for media-integrated and technology-driven performance live streaming, virtual and hybrid producing models data analytics for production planning, budgeting, and audience/venue operations Collaborative and Non-hierarchical Producing Models producing structures for devised and collectively created work, where authorship and decision-making are distributed rather than script-first Sound and Media Design as Producing Practice sound design, projection/media design, and live media integration as both creative and infrastructural components of production Interdisciplinary Partnerships p roducing collaborations across the theatre, dance, music, and visualizati on programs engaging with emerging technology across PVFA This position supports the College's Bachelor of Fine Arts in Theatre, a practice-based, devised-theatre program established in Fall 2024, and complements the program's existing technical design faculty . Successful candidates will teach Stage Management and Producing Theatre and other courses as assigned that fall within their expertise . Preference will be given to candidates who also have experience in sound and/or new media design and an interest in advancing those design dimensions of the program . The successful candidate will be expected to: Establish an active, nationally and internationally visible program of research and/or creative practice investigating production management as a discipline of its own — including its digital, collaborative, and technological dimensions Pursue external funding to support this agenda (e.g., NEA, NEH, foundation, or industry-partnered support for producing technology, digital infrastructure, or interdisciplinary production research) Disseminate work through exhibitions, performances, publications, presentations, software systems, patents, or other recognized scholarly outcomes Contribute production-management expertise to PVFA's theatre curriculum and season, and possibly sound and/or new media design expertise , as a site for applied research and graduate/undergraduate mentorship Build interdisciplinary partnerships that extend production-management research into visualization, data science, or technology domains represented elsewhere in the College Contribute to section, college, and university service About the College The College of Performance, Visualization & Fine Arts was formed in 2022 at Texas A&M University, bringing together the Department of Visualization, the Department of Performance Studies, and the Dance Science Program. PVFA is built without departments. Faculty sections and academic programs sit at the college level, which places musicians, composers, actors, directors, choreographers, dancers, painters, cinematographers, technical artists, level designers, spatial audio engineers, tools programmers, ethnomusicologists, art historians, UI/UX designers, and virtual set designers in one structure rather than in separate units. The College places a heavy emphasis on collaboration and interdisciplinary work in both scholarly and creative research. The College offers the BS, MS, and MFA in Visualization, a PhD in Visual Computing and Interactive Media, and degree programs in music, dance, and theatre. It operates at the main campus in Bryan-College Station and as part of Texas A&M – Fort Worth, with facilities equipped for work at the intersection of the arts and emerging technology. Applicants should consult the college's website ( https://pvfa.tamu.edu/ ) to review our academic and research programs. Qualifications Required Qualifications MFA or PhD in Production Management, Stage Management, Sound Design, Projection/Media Design, or a closely related field. Candidates who are ABD will be considered, provided they demonstrate clear progress forward completion. Demonstrated potential to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research and/or creative practice in production management Demonstrated experience in traditional theatrical production, in addition to collaboratively created work Evidence of engagement with digital/technological, collaborative, or interdisciplinary approaches to producing Preferred Qualifications Research or creative work engaging virtual/hybrid producing, live streaming, data analytics, or other digital production infrastructure Experience with collaborative or non-hierarchical producing models, including devised or collectively created work Expertise in sound design and/or projection/media design as an extension of production-management practice A record of, or clear trajectory toward, external funding for production-technology or interdisciplinary production research Experience building interdisciplinary partnerships across theatre, dance, music, visualization, or technology fields Application Instructions Interested individuals must apply through Interfolio : apply.interfolio.com/195049 and submit the following materials (embed links to your work, student work, etc. where appropriate) : Cover Letter Curriculum vitae Portfolio of creative work Personal Statement: Your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service Names and contact information of five (5) professional references Questions regarding this position may be directed at the Chair of the committee. (George McConnell at gmcconnell@tamu.edu ) Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=190893&JobId=192641": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor or Associate Professor",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor or Associate Professor Psychiatry & Behavioral Sciences Location College Station, Texas Open Date 10/13/2026 Position ID 190893 Description The Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences in the Naresh K. Vashisht College of Medicine at Texas A&M University invites applications for a tenure-track position with an 11-month academic appointment; The anticipated start date for the position is Fall 2026. Applications will be accepted at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, commensurate with qualification. Candidates pursuing internationally recognized research in basic/fundamental or clinical research with a focus on geriatrics, aging, including healthy aging, or neurodegenerative disorders are encouraged to apply. The use of multidisciplinary approaches and research topics that complement those of the existing research groups is an asset. We are particularly interested in self-driven, motivated candidates that can supplement expertise to our current research portfolio that includes work on neural circuitry, stress neurobiology and neuroimmunology, psychiatric genetics and epigenetics, neuroimaging, and clinical trials. Expectations for successful candidates include developing an externally funded research program, teaching graduate and/or medical level courses, and engaging in activities outside teaching and research as part of the department's service expectations. This is an exciting opportunity for new hires to be a part of a rapidly growing department with fundamental, translational, and clinical research focus areas. The Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences in the Naresh K. Vashisht College of Medicine provides many opportunities to recruit trainees through graduate programs in Medical Science ( https://medicine.tamu.edu/medical-sciences/index.html ) and participation in exciting interdepartmental doctoral programs, including Genetics ( https://genetics.tamu.edu/ ) and Neuroscience ( https://tamin.tamu.edu/ ). The position includes competitive startup packages and modern laboratory space. Bryan-College Station, home to Texas A&M University, is a vibrant, dynamic, and rapidly growing community that offers cultural diversity, arts and entertainment, job opportunities, and a high overall quality of life. Located in the heart of the Houston-Dallas-Austin triangle, the region offers the modern amenities of a big city with warm, small-town charm, and its low cost of living makes it an ideal place to live. Qualifications Required: · M.D., Ph.D., M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in relevant field · Postdoctoral experience Preferred: · A demonstrated ability to successfully obtain competitive research funding Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply through the Texas A&M University faculty job board hosted by interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/192641 and upload the following: 1) cover letter, 2) current curriculum vitae, 3) personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching and service, as applicable, and 4) contact information for three references. Questions can be sent to the Chairs of the search committee Adam Case ( acase@tamu.edu ) and/or Shaunna Clark ( slclark@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=185014&JobId=187771": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Artificial Intelligence for Horticultural Crop Systems",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Artificial Intelligence for Horticultural Crop Systems Horticultural Sciences Location College Station, Texas Open Date 6/24/2026 Position ID 185014 Description The Department of Horticultural Sciences in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Artificial Intelligence (AI) for Horticultural Crop Systems. This position is part of a four-position cluster hire in AI in Agriculture in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences aimed at building new research capacity and supporting the development of an undergraduate minor in AI-Enabled Agricultural Systems to prepare students for a rapidly expanding ag-tech workforce. Within this cluster, the Horticultural Sciences position focuses on applying artificial intelligence to address core biological and production challenges in horticultural crops, including but not limited to variable environments, crop load balance, water use, and disease pressure. The position is grounded in plant biology, with an emphasis on understanding and modeling plant growth, yield, quality, and stress responses across a wide range of horticultural productions systems ranging from annual crops to long-lived perennials and tree crops, and integrating plant, environmental, and management processes. This position focuses on the development of models that connect horticultural crop processes with production outcomes and support both discovery and application. These models will improve understanding of plant performance across environments while informing key management decisions such as irrigation scheduling, harvest timing, crop load management, and responses to biotic and abiotic stress. Model development will be supported through the integration of diverse data sources, including but not limited to environmental sensing, canopy imaging, biosensors, genomic data, and image-based phenotyping, combined with machine learning methods that leverage large-scale datasets. These efforts will lead to the development of decision-support systems for horticultural crop production that improve management efficiency, enhance resilience for existing producers, and lower barriers for new entrants to adopt horticultural systems. We are seeking candidates with a strong background in horticulture, plant sciences, or crop systems who also have expertise in artificial intelligence, machine learning (ML), or data analysis. Candidates should be interested in applying AI across scales, from field and greenhouse systems to plant and molecular levels, and in linking data with biological understanding to address both practical production questions and fundamental aspects of plant performance. The successful candidate will establish a nationally and internationally recognized research and teaching program that integrates artificial intelligence with horticultural science to address challenges facing producers, including weather variability, water scarcity, labor constraints, pest and disease pressure, and market uncertainty. Research areas may include, but are not limited to: · Application of AI to address key biological and production challenges in horticultural systems, with a focus on improving decision-making related to crop growth, yield, quality, and resource use to enhance economic returns · Development of models that capture biological and structural variability across crops, from annuals to long-lived perennials and tree crops · Integration of data from environmental sensing, imaging, phenotyping, and genomic sources to understand and predict plant performance · Design of decision-support tools that translate model outputs into actionable strategies for irrigation, crop load management, and stress response The faculty member will collaborate across Texas A&M AgriLife Research, Texas A&M AgriLife Extension, the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, and industry partners. Opportunities exist to engage with major Texas horticultural industries, including viticulture and enology, citrus, pecans, vegetables, controlled environment horticulture, urban horticulture, and ornamental and nursery production, as well as applications in landscape and commercial systems. The successful candidate will be committed to advancing innovation and delivering solutions that support the land-grant mission of Texas A&M University. Major Duties and Responsibilities The successful candidate will develop an innovative, externally funded research program that solves key biological questions in horticultural science by application of artificial intelligence approaches. This includes conducting original research that results in high-impact, peer-reviewed publications and advances sustainable, profitable horticultural systems. The candidate will focus on biological questions of economic importance in horticultural crops and integrate artificial intelligence with data science, sensing technologies, forecasting, and decision frameworks to increase profitability and reduce risks by enhancing water efficiency and sustainable production, disease protection, precision nutrition, and food quality in high-value horticultural crops. The candidate is expected to lead innovations that advance sustainable horticultural production systems that strengthen Texas's economy and enhance nutrition and food quality, thereby promoting healthy living. The candidate will develop and teach new courses, including a three-credit course in Decision-Centric AI for Horticultural Crop Production, which will be part of an 18-credit minor in AI-Enabled Agricultural Systems, emphasizing 'literacy in AI'. As learning outcomes, the students should be able to apply AI and machine learning to address natural resource problems, with attention to uncertainty and responsible use; build and evaluate models using agricultural data streams (field sensors, imagery, genomics/phenomics and economic data); translate model outputs into actionable decisions to improve profitability, sustainability, and resilience; communicate results to technical and non-technical stakeholders and evaluate adoption, risks, and policy considerations. An AI in Agriculture Studio/Practicum (industry/AgriLife project-based capstone) will also be part of the AI minor. Other courses may be assigned as needed by the department. The candidate will mentor graduate students, undergraduate researchers, and postdoctoral scholars, while inspiring the next generation of horticultural scientists to serve Texas and beyond. Service expectations include making contributions to Department, College, and University, as well as actively participating in professional societies. The individual will also be expected to engage with growers, commodity groups, and technology industries across Texas, supporting Texas A&M's land-grant mission to serve the state through teaching, research, and outreach that address real-world challenges. Effort Distribution: 60% research, 30% teaching, and 10% service. The anticipated start date for the position is January 4, 2027. Qualifications Required Qualifications ● A Ph.D. primarily in horticultural sciences, plant sciences, agronomy, biology, or a related field, with additional training in Artificial Intelligence and a strong emphasis on horticultural crops; or a Ph.D. in AI/ML or data sciences must be accompanied with a strong background in plant systems. ● Demonstrated ability or potential to secure extramural funding and develop a productive research program. ● Evidence of effective teaching and mentoring. ● Excellent scientific communication skills Preferred Qualifications ● Experience in studying plant biological questions and applying artificial intelligence technologies in horticultural crops. ● Robust statistical skills and experience in analyzing large datasets and data fusion. ● Engagement in sensor systems, computer vision, robotics, and remote sensing. ● Integration of crop modeling, phenomics and plant physiology. ● Computational skills, including programming languages, coding, software development, and artificial intelligence. ● A proven track record of publications in high-impact, peer-reviewed horticultural research journals. ● Record of interdisciplinary collaborations and/or stakeholder engagement. ● Familiarity with challenges and opportunities relevant to Texas horticulture. Application Instructions Applicants must submit: A cover letter (2-page limit) Curriculum Vitae Personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching & service (single document; 3-page limit) Contact information for three references. Applications will only be accepted online at apply.interfolio.com/187771 . Applications will be reviewed beginning August 10, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date for the position is January 4, 2027. Direct inquiries to: Dr. Isabel Vales, Search Committee Chair Department of Horticulture Sciences Email: isabel.vales@agnet.tamu.edu Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=194206&JobId=195639": {
      "title": "Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Clinical Assistant Professor or Clinical Associate Professor",
      "description": "Back to Listings Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Clinical Assistant Professor or Clinical Associate Professor Periodontics Location Dallas, Texas Open Date 10/21/2026 Position ID 194206 Description The Department of Periodontics at the Texas A&M University College of Dentistry invites applications for a full-time Academic Professional Track (non-tenure) position, with appointment beginning on or after June 1, 2027. Applicants will be considered for the faculty title of Clinical Assistant Professor or Clinical Associate Professor, depending on qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will be expected to participate in supervision of clinical activities such as overseeing and directing patient care, participation in predoctoral dental and undergraduate dental hygiene student didactic and clinical teaching, evaluation of students' performance and professionalism, supervision of student's clinical progress, as well as counseling students regularly. Depending on qualifications and experience, the successful candidate will also be expected to participate in periodontal graduate student didactic and clinical teaching, and mentoring. The candidate will also be expected to actively participate in the department's clinical research activities and requirements, along with the University's and departments' service activities. Depending on qualifications and experience, the faculty hired for this position will be involved in clinical teaching up to 32 hours per week, including didactic teaching, clinical supervision, and clinical teaching, at both pre-doctoral and post-doctoral level. The Department of Periodontics at Texas A&M University College of Dentistry, a dynamic and highly motivated clinical department with an exemplary predoctoral program and one of the premier specialty training programs in the United States, is a recognized leader. The Department is home to a cutting-edge research program with competitive NIH research funding, an international observership program, and a world-renowned Stomatology clinic focusing on mucosal diseases. More information can be found at this link . Qualifications Required qualifications: DDS or equivalent degree. MS/MSc or equivalent degree from a US-accredited program. Certificate in Periodontics from a CODA-accredited periodontal program. Eligible for dental licensure in Texas. For candidates who completed periodontal residency training two or more years prior to applying, board certification by the American Board of Periodontology is required . For candidates who completed periodontal residency training less than two years prior to applying, successful completion of the American Board of Periodontology Qualifying Exam is required . Evidence of clinical research activity. The successful candidate must demo nstrate a record of clinical teaching, mentoring, and service appropriate to their career stage. Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply through the Texas A&M University faculty job board hosted by Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/195639 and upload the following: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) List of three references with full contact information including email addresses. 4) Personal Statement include philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service, as applicable. For questions related to this position please contact Ms. Madison Hardy via email, madison.hardy@tamu.edu or Dr. Dimitris Tatakis via email dntatakis@tamu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=194050&JobId=195490": {
      "title": "Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Lecturer in English (Technical Editing)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Lecturer in English (Technical Editing) English Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/26/2026 Position ID 194050 Description The Department of English in the College of Arts and Sciences at Texas A&M University invites applicants for a full-time, 9-month academic professional track (non-tenure) lecturer in English with a desired specialization in the teaching of technical editing and an anticipated start date of Spring 2027. Lecturers hold non-tenured appointments with primary responsibility for teaching. Teaching assignments will include sections of technical editing, alongside courses in technical writing, freshman composition, and/or advanced writing studies courses, depending on candidate qualification and availability. The normal teaching load for the position is 4/4. Summer teaching for additional compensation is available, subject to departmental need. Texas A&M’s English department is home to an undergraduate major and minor, a minor in health humanities, a professional writing minor, a minor in science fiction studies, and MA and PhD programs. Our course in technical editing is a core component of our growing professional writing minor, and an area of increasing interest across the university. We are looking for a dynamic teacher who can teach students to adapt to the changing contexts, technologies, and demands of advanced writing and editing in various workplaces. About Texas A&M : Texas A&M University is a Hispanic-serving and a First-Gen Forward institution committed to enriching the learning and working environment for all visitors, students, faculty, and staff by promoting a culture that embraces excellence. As a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU) and one of only twenty four universities designated as a land-, sea-, and space-grant institution, Texas A&M combines academic excellence and cutting-edge research with affordability and a commitment to producing academic, business, and civic leaders. Writing and rhetoric faculty have access to a university-wide writing-in-the-disciplines program housed in our University Writing Center at https://writingcenter.tamu.edu/ . Texas A&M is located in College Station, a thriving community known for the quality of its public schools, a strong local economy, and a low cost of living ( https://www.cstx.gov/our_community/recognitions__rankings ). College Station is a two-hour drive from Houston and Austin, and three hours away from San Antonio and Dallas. Qualifications PhD in English or related field is required. Application Instructions To apply please submit a cover letter, CV, personal statement to include philosophy and plans for teaching and service, and contact information for three references to apply.interfolio.com/195490 . Inquiries about the position may be sent to Dr. Mikko Tuhkanen, Search Committee Chair, mikko.tuhkanen@tamu.edu . Interviews will take place through the Zoom platform. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193737&JobId=195201": {
      "title": "Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Open Rank & Title (College Station Campus)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Open Rank & Title (College Station Campus) Construction Science Location College Station, Texas Open Date 10/22/2026 Position ID 193737 Description The Department of Construction Science (COSC), College of Architecture at Texas A&M University (TAMU) invites applications from qualified candidates for a full-time, non-tenure track Academic Professional Track (APT) faculty position with a 9-month academic appointment. This position will be based in College Station, Texas . This is an open-rank search encompassing the titles of Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Principal Lecturer, Instructional Assistant Professor, Instructional Associate Professor, Instructional Professor, or Assistant Professor of the Practice, Associate Professor of the Practice, Professor of the Practice, depending on the applicant's academic qualifications, teaching experience, and breadth of industry experience, depending on the applicant's academic qualifications, teaching experience, and breadth of industry experience. The target start date is Fall 2027. We seek candidates who can demonstrate sustained excellence in teaching, integrate contemporary professional experience into the academic curriculum, promote students’ lifelong learning through high-impact educational practices, and engage in meaningful departmental and industry experience. Responsibilities for this position include teaching at the undergraduate and potentially graduate levels, and service to the department, university, and the field, including outreach to industry. The successful candidate will be expected to teach lecture, lab, and seminar courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The successful candidate will have opportunities for continuing education, professional development, or industry internships during the summer months. Salary is highly competitive and will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s qualifications, experience, and credentials. We invite applications for an Academic Professional Track (APT) faculty position to address critical instructional needs in the Department, focusing on one of three specialized areas: (a) Industrial Construction, (b) Project Controls, Advanced Project Management, and Project Leadership, or (c) Mechanical, Electrical, Plumbing (M/E/P) systems. This position is dedicated primarily to teaching excellence, curriculum development, and service. These domains represent vital pillars for the modern built environment. In the realm of industrial construction, the sector requires educators who can prepare students for the unique complexities of heavy industrial, energy, and process facility projects, emphasizing specialized construction methods, advanced constructability, and large-scale project execution. Simultaneously, as capital projects face unprecedented cost, schedule, and risk pressures, the industry urgently demands deep instructional expertise in project controls, advanced project management, and project leadership, focusing on performance analytics, risk mitigation, schedule and cost optimization, and professional leadership. Furthermore, with the increasing technological sophistication, energy efficiency standards, and building system complexity of modern structures, there is a critical need for expertise in Mechanical, Electrical, and Plumbing (M/E/P) systems to teach students fundamental principles and how to coordinate, install, and manage these essential building systems. We seek candidates who can deliver high-quality instruction, mentor students, and contribute actively to departmental and professional service in either of these critical domains, preparing the next generation of construction professionals for successful careers in the industry. The Department of Construction Science is one of the largest and most renowned programs of construction higher education. The Department offers STEM degrees in all three B.S., M.S. (face-to-face and online options), and Ph.D. levels. The undergraduate program in Construction Science has been continuously accredited by the American Council for Construction Education (ACCE) since 1978, and in 2012, the master’s program in Construction Management was one of the first graduate programs accredited by ACCE. Recently, the department has expanded its academic portfolio. The bachelor’s degree enrolls students at the Higher Education Center at McAllen in addition to at its main campus in College Station, the master’s program was approved for online delivery, and in 2021, the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board (THECB) approved the Ph.D. program in construction science. This doctoral program in construction is the first in Texas and offers three concentration areas; i) construction management and safety, ii) smart construction, and iii) sustainable construction to meet the rapid changes and demands of the construction industry and academia. As of Fall 2026, the Department has a student population of about 1,358 students at the undergraduate level, 143 students at the Master’s degree level, and 52 Ph.D. students; 70 faculty and staff members; the strong support of a Construction Industry Advisory Council (CIAC), composed of over 350 firms from various AEC/FM domains; and eight endowed professorships including the Charles Dewey McMullan Endowed Chair designated for the Department Head. Each year, the Department awards more than $500,000 in scholarships through the support of our partners and conducts the largest department-level career fair at TAMU. On the College Station campus, the Department is housed in Francis Hall, a state-of-the-art 35,000 SF historic building located in the heart of the campus, which is the only stand-alone building for construction education in Texas. More information about the Department is available at: https://www.arch.tamu.edu/cosc/. Qualifications Required Qualifications: A terminal degree (e.g., Ph.D., D.Eng./Eng.D., D.Arch., M.Arch., J.D., or equivalent terminal degree appropriate to the teaching discipline); or A master's degree and significant teaching experience at the college level in the field or a related field appropriate to the discipline in which the faculty member will teach (e.g., Construction Science, Construction Management, Engineering, Architecture, Business, Education, or a closely related field); or A bachelor's degree in a discipline related to the field in which the faculty member will teach and an extraordinary record of accomplishment in an applied or professional setting, demonstrated through a combination of significant industry leadership, professional licensure or certifications (recognized industry or professional credentials directly related to the faculty member’s teaching discipline, (e.g., P.E., RA, CPC, PMP, LEED AP, CCM ), executive-level experience, national or international professional recognition, major project accomplishments, industry awards, published professional work, or other evidence of sustained distinction in the profession. Preferred Qualifications: A minimum of three years of construction science industry experience in the U.S. Demonstrated expertise and a successful teaching record in core construction science and management topics including construction scheduling, construction materials and methods, mechanical electrical and plumbing systems, structures, and related topics . Application Instructions Application materials must be submitted online at apply.interfolio.com/195201 The review of applications will begin during the first week of December 2026; however, the positions will remain open until filled. To ensure full consideration, applicants are strongly encouraged to submit their applications by November 15, 2026 , as applications received by that date will receive priority review. Applications must include the following: Cover Letter A curriculum vita/resume Personal statement to include philosophy and plans for teaching and/or service, as applicable At least three professional references with complete contact information Questions regarding this position should be sent to Mrs. Liz Smith ( liz-smith@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193712&JobId=195180": {
      "title": "Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Open Rank & Title (McAllen Campus)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Open Rank & Title (McAllen Campus) Construction Science Location McAllen, Texas Open Date 10/22/2026 Position ID 193712 Description The Department of Construction Science (COSC), College of Architecture at Texas A&M University (TAMU) invites applications from qualified candidates for a full-time, non-tenure track Academic Professional Track (APT) faculty position with a 9-month academic appointment. This position will be based in McAllen, Texas and will support the department’s programs at the Texas A&M Higher Education Center at McAllen . This is an open-rank search encompassing the titles of Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Principal Lecturer, Instructional Assistant Professor, Instructional Associate Professor, Instructional Professor, or Assistant Professor of the Practice, Associate Professor of the Practice, Professor of the Practice, depending on the applicant's academic qualifications, teaching experience, and breadth of industry experience, depending on the applicant's academic qualifications, teaching experience, and breadth of industry experience. The target start date is Fall 2027. We seek candidates who can demonstrate sustained excellence in teaching, integrate contemporary professional experience into the academic curriculum, promote students’ lifelong learning through high-impact educational practices, and engage in meaningful departmental and industry experience. Responsibilities for this position include teaching at the undergraduate and potentially graduate levels, and service to the department, university, and the field, including outreach to industry. The successful candidate will be expected to teach lecture, lab, and seminar courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The successful candidate will have opportunities for continuing education, professional development, or industry internships during the summer months. Salary is highly competitive and will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s qualifications, experience, and credentials. We invite candidates who have expertise in construction scheduling, construction materials and methods, structures, mechanical electrical and plumbing systems, and related topics and who can demonstrate sustained excellence in teaching, integrate contemporary professional experience into the academic curriculum, promote students’ lifelong learning through high-impact educational practices, and engage in meaningful departmental and industry service. The Department of Construction Science is one of the largest and most renowned programs of construction higher education. The Department offers STEM degrees in all three B.S., M.S. (face-to-face and online options), and Ph.D. levels. The undergraduate program in Construction Science has been continuously accredited by the American Council for Construction Education (ACCE) since 1978, and in 2012, the master’s program in Construction Management was one of the first graduate programs accredited by ACCE. Recently, the department has expanded its academic portfolio. The bachelor’s degree enrolls students at the Higher Education Center at McAllen in addition to at its main campus in College Station, the master’s program was approved for online delivery, and in 2021, the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board (THECB) approved the Ph.D. program in construction science. This doctoral program in construction is the first in Texas and offers three concentration areas; i) construction management and safety, ii) smart construction, and iii) sustainable construction to meet the rapid changes and demands of the construction industry and academia. As of Fall, 2026 the Department has a student population composed of 1,358 students at the undergraduate level, 143 students at the Master’s degree level, and 52 Ph.D. students; 70 faculty and staff members; the strong support of a Construction Industry Advisory Council (CIAC), composed of over 350 firms from various AEC/FM domains; and eight endowed professorships including the Charles Dewey McMullan Endowed Chair designated for the Department Head. Each year, the Department awards more than $500,000 in scholarships through the support of our partners and conducts the largest department-level career fair at TAMU. On the College Station campus, the department is housed in Francis Hall, a state-of-the-art 35,000 SF historic building located in the heart of the campus, which is the only stand-alone building for construction education in Texas. More information about the Department is available at: https://www.arch.tamu.edu/cosc/. Located in the heart of the Rio Grande Valley , the Texas A&M University Higher Education Center McAllen (HECM) offers the chance to live and work in one of Texas’s fastest-growing and most dynamic regions. As a teaching-focused campus, HECM emphasizes high-quality instruction and values faculty who are passionate about student success. Serving a student body that is majority first-generation with an average student-to-faculty ratio of about 12:1 across most majors, faculty here enjoy close student engagement and meaningful opportunities for impact. McAllen and neighboring Edinburg are known as “the cities of festivals,” celebrating music, art, and heritage year-round—from the Fiestas de Palmas to the Texas Citrus Fiesta and Hidalgo’s Festival of Lights . Both cities offer short commutes and an affordable cost of living. Just an hour from the Gulf of Mexico’s South Padre Island , faculty can spend weekends on the beach, bird-watching at the World Birding Center , or exploring local treasures like McAllen’s International Museum of Art & Science ( IMAS ), the Gelman Stained Glass Museum , and nearby Gladys Porter Zoo . The region also stands at the forefront of innovation, with SpaceX Starbase just a short drive away in Boca Chica. McAllen’s nationally recognized food scene—hailed by Texas Highways as one of the hottest in the state —offers everything from authentic regional Mexican cuisine to chef-driven fine dining. For faculty seeking a close-knit campus community and an unmatched blend of cultural richness, affordability, and opportunity, McAllen and the Rio Grande Valley offer a truly exceptional place to live, teach, and grow. Qualifications Required Qualifications: A terminal degree (e.g., Ph.D., D.Eng./Eng.D., D.Arch., M.Arch., J.D., or equivalent terminal degree appropriate to the teaching discipline) or A master's degree and significant teaching experience at the college level in the field or a related field appropriate to the discipline in which the faculty member will teach (e.g., Construction Science, Construction Management, Engineering, Architecture, Business, Education, or a closely related field); or A bachelor's degree in a discipline related to the field in which the faculty member will teach and an extraordinary record of accomplishment in an applied or professional setting, demonstrated through a combination of significant industry leadership, professional licensure or certifications (recognized industry or professional credentials directly related to the faculty member’s teaching discipline, (e.g., P.E., RA, CPC, PMP, LEED AP, CCM) executive-level experience, national or international professional recognition, major project accomplishments, industry awards, published professional work, or other evidence of sustained distinction in the profession. Preferred Qualifications: A minimum of three years of construction science industry experience in the U.S. Demonstrated expertise and a successful teaching record in core construction science and management topics including construction scheduling, construction materials and methods, mechanical electrical and plumbing systems, structures, and related topics . Application Instructions Application materials must be submitted online at apply.interfolio.com/195180 The review of applications will begin during the first week of December 2026; however, the positions will remain open until filled. To ensure full consideration, applicants are strongly encouraged to submit their applications by November 15, 2026 , as applications received by that date will receive priority review. Applications must include the following: Cover Letter A curriculum vita/resume Personal statement to include philosophy and plans for teaching and/or service, as applicable At least three professional references with complete contact information Questions regarding this position should be sent to Mrs. Liz Smith ( liz-smith@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=193799&JobId=195258": {
      "title": "Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Open Rank and Title",
      "description": "Back to Listings Academic Professional Track (Non-Tenure): Open Rank and Title Finance Location College Station, TX Open Date 10/21/2026 Position ID 193799 Description The Adam C. Sinn ’00 Department of Finance at Mays Business School, Texas A&M University, invites applications for three Academic Professional Track (non-tenure) positions with a nine-month academic appointment. Applicants will be considered for the faculty titles of (Visiting) Lecturer, (Visiting) Senior Lecturer, (Visiting) Principal Lecturer, (Visiting) Clinical Assistant Professor, (Visiting) Clinical Associate Professor (Visiting) Clinical Professor, or (Visiting) Assistant Professor of the Practice, (Visiting) Associate Professor of the Practice, (Visiting) Professor of the Practice, depending on experience and qualifications. Full-time and part-time (visiting) appointments are possible, beginning Spring or Fall 2027. The successful applicant will be responsible for the organization, delivery, evaluation and assessment of finance courses and student outcomes associated with those courses. Qualifications For appointments at the \"Lecturer\" level including (Visiting) Lecturer, (Visiting) Senior Lecturer, (Visiting) Principal Lecturer : Applicants must have a PhD appropriate for the field in which the faculty member will teach or master's level degree appropriate for the field in which the faculty member will teach and significant teaching experience at the college level in the field or in a related field. For appointments at the \"Clinical\" level including (Visiting) Clinical Assistant Professor, (Visiting) Clinical Associate Professor, (Visiting) Clinical Professor : Applicants must have a PhD in finance, economics, or a closely related field. A record of excellence in teaching and publications in peer-reviewed academic journals is preferred. Appointments at the \"of the Practice\" level including (Visiting) Assistant Professor of the Practice, (Visiting) Associate Professor of the Practice, (Visiting) Professor of the Practice : Applicants must have a Master’s Degree in the area that relates to their teaching and service and significant teaching experience at the college level in the field or in a related field or have an extraordinary record of accomplishment in an applied setting. Application Instructions We will begin reviewing candidates immediately and continue reviewing as applications are received and until the positions are filled. Interested applicants must apply through the Texas A&M University faculty job board hosted by Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/195258 and upload the following: 1) Cover Letter, 2) Curriculum Vitae, 3) Personal Statement: Your statement should include your philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service as applicable, and 4) Names and contact information of four (4) professional references For questions please contact Jamie Brown at jrbrown@tamu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=182050&JobId=185195": {
      "title": "Director, Texas A&M Energy Institute, with appointment as Associate Professor or Professor",
      "description": "Back to Listings Director, Texas A&M Energy Institute, with appointment as Associate Professor or Professor Division of Research Location College Station, TX Open Date 5/5/2026 Position ID 182050 Description Texas A&M University invites applications for the position of Director of the Texas A&M Energy Institute, with appointment as a tenured Associate Professor or Professor. F ounded in 2009, t he Texas A&M Energy Institute ( http://energy.tamu.edu ) is a joint institute between Texas A&M University and the Texas A&M Engineering Experiment Station (TEES). The Director serves as the chief academic and administrative officer of the Institute and reports to the Texas A&M University Vice President for Research. The Texas A&M Energy Institute pursues and supports new approaches for multi-disciplinary energy research, education, and external partnerships. These approaches cross departmental and college boundaries and address all facets of the energy landscape that naturally connect engineering, sciences, technologies, economics, law, and policy. Dedicated to discovering energy solutions and improving the quality of life, the Texas A&M Energy Institute engages undergraduate and graduate students, postdoctoral associates, research staff, and faculty members toward the study and development of innovative technologies and policies for energy production and energy conservation, fostering an integrated approach and research agenda across disciplines and developing quantitative tools for the analysis of energy systems. Beyond research, it aims to educate the next generation of leaders in energy through its innovative Master of Science in Energy and Certificate in Energy educational programs. The Energy Institute engages in interdisciplinary research and scholarship with more than 320 faculty affiliates from nine colleges and schools at Texas A&M University, more than 20 Texas A&M University departments, two Texas A&M University branch campuses, and six Texas A&M University System member institutions. The Director's responsibilities include: Guiding the development and implementation of a strategic vision and long-term goals for the Energy Institute, focusing on addressing complex, multi-faceted global challenges. Working to create a community of energy scholars across the Texas A&M University System, fostering and engaging Energy Institute faculty affiliates, and carrying forward the vision for interdisciplinary engagement across the university. Closely following new trends and emerging fields in energy innovation, and through engagement with Texas A&M leadership, identifying areas where Texas A&M can successfully position itself for funding opportunities and collaborations. Engaging external entities, stakeholders, and industries at the local, state, national, and international levels to promote the vision, goals, and activities of the Energy Institute. Developing and expanding an institute research portfolio that leverages internal and external networks for competitive extramural research funding. Overseeing the Interdisciplinary Curricular Program in Energy, including the Master of Science in Energy and Certificate in Energy academic programs. Providing leadership in energy education across Texas A&M, including professional development and continuing education curriculum offered by the institute. Working to ensure resources are secured and professionally managed for the smooth operation of the Energy Institute, including from external awards and business and industry collaborations. Serving as an internal and external focal point for identifying and connecting with energy expertise at Texas A&M. Overseeing the management, recruitment, evaluation, and professional development of academic and research staff within the institute, fostering a collaborative and high-performance culture aligned with the university's values. Ensuring compliance with university, state, and federal policies and regulations. Qualifications Minimum/Required Qualifications: Candidates must possess a Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in an energy-related scientific, engineering, economic, or policy discipline. Candidates must demonstrate qualifications and experience appropriate for appointment as an Associate Professor or Professor in an appropriate academic department at Texas A&M University. Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate qualifications and experience appropriate for appointment as Professor in an appropriate academic department at Texas A&M University, including a distinguished record of scholarly research, teaching, and externally funded research. The ideal candidate will also demonstrate: A strong record of interdisciplinary collaboration across academic disciplines and institutional boundaries. Leadership experience in research administration, academic programs, or large-scale collaborative initiatives. Success in securing and managing competitive extramural funding. A demonstrated commitment to advancing energy research, education, and innovation through partnerships with academia, industry, and government. Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/185195 and submit the following required materials: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter (maximum three pages) describing the applicant’s interest in the position, relevant experience, and vision for the position. Personal Statement describing the applicant’s philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service, as applicable. Four (4) Confidential Letters of Recommendation (requested through the applicant’s Interfolio account) Review of applications will take place July 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Questions regarding the position or application process may be directed to Larry Elliott at larry.elliott@tamu.edu . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    },
    "https://faculty.tamu.edu/JobDetail.aspx?PositionId=194046&JobId=195493": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (College Station Campus)",
      "description": "Back to Listings Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor (College Station Campus) Construction Science Location College Station, Texas Open Date 10/22/2026 Position ID 194046 Description The Department of Construction Science (COSC), College of Architecture at Texas A&M University (TAMU) invites applications from qualified candidates for one (1) full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position , with a 9-month academic appointment with a target start date of Fall 2027. This position will be in College Station, Texas. We seek candidates who can develop and grow a highly visible and nationally recognized research program in their area of specialty, contribute to scholarly work in the construction domain, promote students’ lifelong learning through the integration of research and education, and engage in professional service. Responsibilities for this position include teaching at the graduate and undergraduate levels, conducting impactful research for the construction industry and the society through competitive grants and contracts, and service to the department, university, and the field, including outreach to industry. We seek candidates who have strong expertise on one of two critical areas of need for the construction industry: (a) Industrialized Construction and Advanced Automation, or (b) Advanced Project Delivery and Management. Both domains address pivotal challenges facing the modern built environment. On one front, the construction industry is undergoing a transformative shift toward off-site manufacturing, modular construction, and AI-integrated robotics to overcome long-standing productivity and efficiency constraints. On another, as capital projects grow in scale, technological complexity, and risk, the industry urgently requires sophisticated frameworks in project delivery, performance analytics, risk management, and data-driven decision support. We seek candidates who can develop and grow a highly visible, nationally recognized education and research program in either of these vital areas. This faculty role will spearhead pioneering research and innovative educational strategies, preparing the next generation of professionals to lead the industry toward smart, automated, sustainable construction systems or high-performance project delivery. The Department of Construction Science is one of the largest and most renowned programs of construction higher education. The Department offers STEM degrees in all three B.S., M.S. (face-to-face and online options), and Ph.D. levels. The undergraduate program in Construction Science has been continuously accredited by the American Council for Construction Education (ACCE) since 1978, and in 2012, the master’s program in Construction Management was one of the first graduate programs accredited by ACCE. Recently, the department has expanded its academic portfolio. The bachelor’s degree enrolls students at the Higher Education Center at McAllen in addition to its main campus in College Station, the master’s program was approved for online delivery, and in 2021, the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board (THECB) approved the Ph.D. program in construction science. This doctoral program in construction is the first in Texas and offers three concentration areas; i) construction management and safety, ii) smart construction, and iii) sustainable construction to meet the rapid changes and demands of the construction industry and academia. As of Fall 2026, the Department has a student population of 1,358 students at the undergraduate level, 143 students at the Master’s degree level, and 52 Ph.D. students; 70 faculty and staff members; the strong support of a Construction Industry Advisory Council (CIAC), composed of over 350 firms from various AEC/FM domains; and eight endowed professorships including the Charles Dewey McMullan Endowed Chair designated for the Department Head. Each year, the Department awards more than $500,000 in scholarships through the support of our partners and conducts the largest department-level career fair at TAMU. The Department is housed in Francis Hall, a state-of-the-art 35,000 SF historic building located in the heart of the campus, which is the only stand-alone building for construction education in Texas. More information about the Department is available at: https://www.arch.tamu.edu/cosc/. Located in College Station, Texas, TAMU is about 90 miles northwest of Houston, 100 miles northeast of Austin, and within a three-hour drive to Dallas, San Antonio, and Galveston. TAMU’s main campus is home to over 75,000 students, with more than 528,000 former students worldwide. As an incubator for knowledge creation and an exemplar in experiential teaching, TAMU is a Tier 1 public university, a member of the Association of American Universities (AAU), and one of only 24 institutions in the nation to hold the triple designation as a land-grant, sea-grant, and space-grant university. More information on TAMU is available at: https://www.tamu.edu /. Qualifications Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. degree in construction or a closely related discipline. Candidates who have completed all Ph.D. requirements except the dissertation (ABD) will be considered, provided they demonstrate clear progress toward completion. Demonstrated evidence of or a strong potential to develop, a high-impact research program supported by external funding and collaborate across disciplines to develop research initiatives in their area of expertise. Commitment to excellence in teaching in both graduate and undergraduate levels, and mentoring of undergraduates, graduate students, and post-docs. Preferred Qualifications: A minimum of three years of industry experience in the U.S. Expertise and a successful research/teaching record in core construction management areas including construction materials and methods, scheduling, mechanical electrical and plumbing systems, structures, and project management. Proven expertise in construction workforce development including a strong background in advanced pedagogical methods, recruitment and retention strategies, and related educational research. Demonstrated ability or vision for designing and implementing innovative workforce development and professional training programs for the construction industry. Application Instructions Application materials must be submitted online at apply.interfolio.com/195493 . The review of applications will begin the first week of December, but the positions will remain open until filled. For best consideration, applications should be submitted by November 15, 2026. Applications must include the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Personal statement to include philosophy and plans for research, teaching, and service, as applicable (maximum 3 pages in total). Names and contact information of up to five professional references Questions regarding this position should be sent to Mrs. Liz Smith ( liz-smith@tamu.edu ). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Opportunity/Veterans/Disability Employer. The University is aware that attracting and retaining exceptional faculty often depends on meeting the needs of two careers and therefore has a Dual Career Program ."
    }
  },
